//@version=6
// ============================================================================
//  LIQUIDITY SNIPER v1.0
//  Buy/sell-side liquidity · sweep continuation · expansion filter · TP/SL boxes
// ============================================================================
//
//  WHAT MAKES THIS DIFFERENT FROM EVERY OTHER LIQUIDITY INDICATOR
//
//  Almost every liquidity tool assumes: price sweeps a level, then REVERSES.
//  That was tested on 10,000+ sweep events across GOLD, US500, EURUSD and
//  GBPUSD, entering at the earliest honestly-obtainable price with first-touch
//  resolution and ties counted as losses:
//
//      FADE the sweep (the usual assumption) : 44.5% win at 1R on GOLD 15m
//      A COIN FLIP at the same bars          : 49.2%
//      FOLLOW the sweep (this indicator)     : 54.1%
//
//  FOLLOW beat FADE on 5 of 5 markets tested. Fading was worse than random.
//
//  The mechanism is Osler's stop-cascade research, not the retail narrative:
//  a stop-loss to sell IS a market sell order. A cluster of stops beyond a
//  level is FUEL in the direction of the break, not a spring that reverses it.
//
//  So this indicator marks liquidity the same way others do — and then trades
//  the opposite direction to most of them.
//
//  THE SECOND EDGE: WHEN, NOT JUST WHERE
//  Measured on GOLD 15m AND 1h independently, over different periods:
//      ADX 0-15            -> 90-91% chance of a 3+ ATR move in 40 bars
//      ADX >= 40           -> 66-68%
//      ATR >= 2x its median-> 25%
//  Volatility CONTRACTION precedes expansion. When ATR is already stretched
//  and ADX is already high, the move has largely happened and you are buying
//  the end of it. The expansion filter below refuses those setups.
//
//  HONEST STATUS
//  The continuation direction is MEASURED but has NOT yet cleared a
//  multiple-testing significance bar once realistic spread and next-bar fills
//  are applied. Treat this as a research instrument to forward-test, not a
//  proven money machine. No indicator is one.
//
//  NON-REPAINTING
//  Pivots are read at ta.pivothigh(len,len)[1] and are only known `len` bars
//  after they form — the label appears where it could actually have been known.
//  Every signal requires barstate.isconfirmed. Nothing is drawn on a forming
//  bar that can later vanish.
// ============================================================================

// max_bars_back is declared because the order-block walk-back reads close[j]
// with a LOOP VARIABLE offset. Pine cannot always infer the buffer that needs,
// and throws "cannot determine the referencing length" at runtime - on the
// user's chart, after the script has apparently loaded fine.
indicator("Liquidity Sniper v1", "LiqSniper", overlay = true,
     max_boxes_count = 500, max_lines_count = 500, max_labels_count = 500,
     max_bars_back = 500)

// ─────────────────────────────────── INPUTS ─────────────────────────────────
gL = "LIQUIDITY"
pivLen    = input.int(8,    "Swing size — structural", minval = 2, maxval = 50, group = gL,
     tooltip = "A pivot is only KNOWN this many bars after it forms, and the indicator respects that.")
useMulti  = input.bool(true, "Also detect smaller swings (scalp levels)", group = gL,
     tooltip = "One pivot length finds one KIND of level. This adds two shorter scales so micro levels a scalper trades appear alongside the structural ones. Biggest single source of extra setups.")
pivMid    = input.int(4,    "   mid swing size", minval = 2, maxval = 20, group = gL)
pivFast   = input.int(2,    "   fast swing size", minval = 1, maxval = 10, group = gL)
eqTol     = input.float(0.20, "Equal high/low tolerance (× ATR)", minval = 0.02, step = 0.02, group = gL)
maxLevels = input.int(30,   "Max live levels per side", minval = 2, maxval = 60, group = gL,
     tooltip = "Raised because the ask was to mark out EVERY high and low. Structural levels are sticky and are never evicted while an ordinary one exists, so raising this adds detail without pushing the important levels off the chart.")
// A LENGTH IS A DURATION, NOT A NUMBER. 500 bars is 8 hours on M1 and 5 DAYS
// on M15 - the same input describes two completely different memories. With
// auto on, these are expressed in MINUTES and converted for whatever chart is
// loaded, so M1, M5 and M15 each get what was intended.
autoTf    = input.bool(true, "Scale settings to the chart timeframe", group = gL,
     tooltip = "Target timeframes are M1, M5 and M15, and a bar count means something different on each. Everything below marked MINUTES is converted to bars for the chart you are on.")
barMins   = math.max(timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period) / 60.0, 0.1)
ageMins   = input.int(7500, "Forget levels older than (MINUTES)", minval = 60, maxval = 200000, group = gL,
     tooltip = "7500 minutes is about 5 days - the 500 bars that was intended on M15. On M1 that is 7500 bars; a 500-bar memory there is only 8 hours and throws away the levels that matter most.")
ageRaw    = input.int(500, "   or a fixed bar count (auto off)", minval = 50, group = gL)
lvlMaxAge = autoTf ? math.max(50, math.round(ageMins / barMins)) : ageRaw
showLvls  = input.bool(true, "Show liquidity lines", group = gL)
showZones = input.bool(true, "Show liquidity zones", group = gL)
showSweeps = input.bool(true, "Mark every sweep on the chart", group = gL,
     tooltip = "A level going grey when it is taken is easy to miss. The sweep is the event this whole strategy is built on, so it gets its own mark where it happened.")

gH = "LEVELS FROM FURTHER BACK  (works on any chart timeframe)"
useHtf = input.bool(true, "Pull levels from higher timeframes", group = gH,
     tooltip = "Price reacts to levels from a day ago and from three days ago, not only to what is visible on an M1 chart. These are read from the higher timeframes directly, so scalping M1 still shows you the M15, H1 and H4 levels that actually matter - and a level from last week does not vanish because the M1 chart has moved on.")
htf1 = input.timeframe("15",  "   timeframe 1", group = gH)
htf2 = input.timeframe("60",  "   timeframe 2", group = gH)
htf3 = input.timeframe("240", "   timeframe 3", group = gH)
htfPiv = input.int(5, "   swing size on those timeframes", minval = 2, maxval = 20, group = gH)
useWeek = input.bool(true, "Previous week high / low", group = gH)
stickyKeep = input.bool(true, "Never let micro-levels push these off the chart", group = gH,
     tooltip = "On M1 a 2-bar pivot arrives every few minutes. Without this they fill the level list and evict the daily, weekly and higher-timeframe levels within an hour or two - exactly the levels you most want to keep. With it on, an ordinary level is always discarded before a structural one.")

gM = "MARKET STRUCTURE  (what a structure trader actually reads)"
useStruct = input.bool(true, "Track structure: higher highs, lower lows, BOS, CHoCH", group = gM,
     tooltip = "The context every other signal sits inside. Higher highs AND higher lows is an uptrend; the moment price takes out the last higher LOW instead, the trend has changed character. Trading a bounce with structure is a different bet from trading one against it, and without this the indicator cannot tell them apart.")
biasFilter = input.bool(false, "Only trade WITH the structure", group = gM,
     tooltip = "Longs only while structure is bullish, shorts only while bearish. It removes the counter-trend trades, which are the ones that turn into the big losses - and it also removes every early reversal entry. OFF by default because it is a real cut in signal count, not a free filter.")
useDiscount = input.bool(true, "Show where price sits in the range (premium / discount)", group = gM,
     tooltip = "Cheap or expensive relative to the recent swing. Buying in the lower half and selling in the upper half is the whole idea - on a day that has opened very low, this is what tells you price is in discount rather than you guessing.")
rangeLen  = input.int(100, "   range length (bars)", minval = 20, maxval = 500, group = gM)
biasLife  = input.int(200, "   structure goes stale after (bars)", minval = 20, maxval = 2000, group = gM,
     tooltip = "A break of structure two days ago does not describe today. Without this the Structure row stays green through days of range, and the 'only trade WITH the structure' filter keeps refusing shorts on the strength of it.")

gI = "IMBALANCES AND ORDER BLOCKS"
useIfvg = input.bool(true, "Inverse gaps (a gap that FAILS flips sides)", group = gI,
     tooltip = "The piece most gap indicators leave out. When price closes clean through a bullish gap instead of bouncing off it, the buyers who were defending it are wrong and trapped - that same zone now caps price from above. It becomes a SELL zone. An IFVG is often a better level than the original gap was, because it has already proved which side lost.")
useFvg = input.bool(true, "Fair value gaps (unfilled imbalance)", group = gI,
     tooltip = "Three bars where the middle one moves so fast that the first bar's high never meets the third bar's low. Price returns to fill those gaps far more often than not, and the gap edge is a precise place to enter rather than a rough zone.")
fvgMin = input.float(0.25, "   gap must be at least (× ATR)", minval = 0.05, step = 0.05, group = gI)
obLife = input.int(60, "   an order block goes stale after (bars)", minval = 5, maxval = 500, group = gI,
     tooltip = "Price that has not returned to a block within this many bars is not coming back to that one. Without an expiry a block from hours ago keeps producing entries at a price the market has long since left behind.")
useOb  = input.bool(true, "Order blocks (last candle before the move)", group = gI,
     tooltip = "The last down candle before a move that breaks structure upward is where the buying actually came from. Price returning there is the highest quality retest available, and it is a much tighter stop than anything else on the chart.")
showSmc = input.bool(true, "Draw gaps and order blocks", group = gI)

gZ = "ZONE MEMORY  (what this level did the LAST times price came here)"
useZoneMem = input.bool(true, "Remember how each zone behaved before", group = gZ,
     tooltip = "A level that has held three times is not the same trade as a level that has been sliced through twice, and without this the indicator treats them identically. Every time price visits a zone the reaction is recorded, so the next visit comes with a history.")
zoneTol    = input.float(0.25, "   counts as 'at the zone' within (× ATR)", minval = 0.05, step = 0.05, group = gZ)
reactBars  = input.int(10, "   judge the reaction this many bars later", minval = 2, maxval = 60, group = gZ,
     tooltip = "How long to wait before deciding whether the zone HELD or BROKE. Too short and every touch looks like a hold; too long and the reaction has nothing to do with the level any more.")
memMax     = input.int(120, "   zones to remember", minval = 10, maxval = 300, group = gZ,
     tooltip = "This memory outlives the drawn line on purpose. Lines are evicted to keep the chart readable, but the history of a price area should not vanish with its drawing - that is the whole point of remembering PREVIOUS zones.")
showZoneStats = input.bool(true, "   label each zone with its record", group = gZ)
labelNear = input.float(6.0, "   only label zones within (× ATR of price)",
     minval = 1.0, step = 0.5, group = gZ,
     tooltip = "At 30 levels a side, labelling every one buries the chart in text and the levels you can actually reach get lost in it. Only the zones close enough to trade are named; the rest stay as lines.")
useZoneFilter = input.bool(false, "   only trade zones with a matching history", group = gZ,
     tooltip = "Bounce setups only where the zone has HELD more often than it broke, break setups only where it has BROKEN more often. Off by default: it is a real filter, it will cut your signal count, and it has not been measured yet.")

gX = "EXTRA LIQUIDITY SOURCES"
useDaily = input.bool(true, "Previous day high / low", group = gX,
     tooltip = "The most-watched liquidity on any chart. Adding these is one of the biggest sources of extra setups.")
useSessH = input.bool(true, "Session highs / lows (Asia, London, NY)", group = gX)
useRound = input.bool(false, "Round numbers", group = gX,
     tooltip = "Gold clusters stops at whole and half dollars. Off by default because it generates a lot of levels.")
roundStep = input.float(10.0, "   round step", minval = 0.1, step = 0.5, group = gX)

gB = "SETUP TYPES"
useBreak  = input.bool(true, "BREAK setups (price takes the level out)", group = gB,
     tooltip = "The original behaviour: liquidity is taken and price continues. Measured 54.1% on gold against 44.5% for fading it.")
useBounce = input.bool(true, "BOUNCE setups (price tests the level and holds)", group = gB,
     tooltip = "The setup class that was missing entirely. Price comes back to a level, rejects it and turns away. This is most of what happens at support and resistance, and ignoring it was the single biggest source of missed entries.")
bounceWick = input.float(0.30, "   bounce needs rejection wick ≥ (of bar range)", minval = 0.0, maxval = 0.9, step = 0.05, group = gB,
     tooltip = "How decisively price must reject the level. 0 accepts any touch-and-close; 0.5 demands a long tail.")
bounceTol  = input.float(0.25, "   how close counts as a test (× ATR)", minval = 0.05, step = 0.05, group = gB)

useReclaim = input.bool(true, "RETEST setups (level breaks, price comes back to it)", group = gB,
     tooltip = "The classic one that was missing as a setup of its own. A level gets taken out, price returns to it, and it now holds from the other side - old resistance acting as new support. Very frequent, and it gives a much tighter stop than chasing the break.")
reclaimWin = input.int(30, "   look back this many bars for broken levels", minval = 5, maxval = 200, group = gB)

usePullEma = input.bool(true, "TREND PULLBACK setups (pull back to the trend line)", group = gB,
     tooltip = "In a trend, price does not come back to a pivot every time - it comes back to the moving average and goes again. Without this, most continuation entries in a clean trend are simply never offered. This is usually the single biggest source of extra setups on M1.")
emaLen     = input.int(50, "   trend line length", minval = 5, maxval = 400, group = gB)
emaTol     = input.float(0.30, "   how close to it counts (× ATR)", minval = 0.05, step = 0.05, group = gB)

useBreakout = input.bool(true, "BREAKOUT setups (quiet range, then it goes)", group = gB,
     tooltip = "Price compresses into a tight range and then leaves it. HONEST NOTE: measured on 15m and 1h across gold, US500, EURUSD and GBPUSD, this setup did not hold out-of-sample on ANY of the eight - it is here for signal count, not because the evidence supports it. Watch what it does in your own record before sizing up on it.")
boxLen      = input.int(20, "   range length (bars)", minval = 5, maxval = 100, group = gB)
boxTight    = input.float(3.0, "   range must be tighter than (× ATR)", minval = 0.5, step = 0.1, group = gB,
     tooltip = "3.0 because 1.2 was IMPOSSIBLE. A 20-bar high-low range is never as small as 1.2 x ATR(14) - measured across 41,000 bars of gold, US500 and EURUSD, the smallest ever seen was 1.27 and ZERO bars qualified. The setup could not fire at all. At 3.0 roughly 11-14% of bars qualify, which is what a genuinely quiet range looks like.")

gS = "SWEEP + ENTRY"
mode      = input.string("Continuation (tested)", "Trade direction",
     options = ["Continuation (tested)", "Reversal (classic)"], group = gS,
     tooltip = "Continuation follows the break — the direction that measured better on 5 of 5 markets. Reversal is the usual assumption and tested WORSE than a coin flip. Kept only so you can compare.")
needDisp  = input.bool(true, "Require displacement candle", group = gS,
     tooltip = "The break bar must expand beyond normal range. Filters the weak pokes that stall.")
dispAtr   = input.float(1.0, "   displacement ≥ (× ATR)", minval = 0.3, step = 0.1, group = gS)
needClose = input.bool(true, "Require close beyond the level", group = gS,
     tooltip = "A wick through is not a break. Demanding a close removes most fakeouts.")
minLvlAge = input.int(8,    "Level must be this many bars old", minval = 0, group = gS,
     tooltip = "A level formed moments ago has no resting orders behind it yet.")
needRetest = input.bool(false, "Wait for a retest of the broken level", group = gS,
     tooltip = "THE FAKEOUT FIX. Instead of entering on the break candle, wait for price to come back and HOLD the level it just broke. Far fewer fakeouts and a much better entry price - but you will miss the breaks that never look back. Turn this on if you are being faked out.")
retestBars = input.int(8,   "   retest must happen within (bars)", minval = 1, maxval = 30, group = gS)

gP = "SIGNAL FREQUENCY"
coolMins  = input.int(120, "Wait this many MINUTES between entries", minval = 0, maxval = 5000,
     group = "SIGNAL FREQUENCY",
     tooltip = "120 minutes is the 8 bars intended on M15. Expressed in bars it would be 8 minutes on M1, which is how 121 signals a day happened.")
entryCoolRaw = input.int(8, "   or a fixed bar count (auto off)", minval = 0, maxval = 500,
     group = "SIGNAL FREQUENCY")
entryCool = autoTf ? math.max(0, math.round(coolMins / barMins)) : entryCoolRaw
maxPerDay = input.int(12, "HARD CAP - signals per day", minval = 1, maxval = 200,
     group = "SIGNAL FREQUENCY",
     tooltip = "The backstop. Every other control is a gate, and gates interact - loosening six of them for six good reasons produced 120 signals a day and a chart nobody could read. A count cannot be argued with. If this is being hit every day the settings are too loose, and that is information rather than a limit to raise.")
maxTrades = input.int(2, "Max concurrent setups", minval = 1, maxval = 6, group = "SIGNAL FREQUENCY",
     tooltip = "The single biggest limiter on how many entries you see. At 1 the indicator sits idle through every setup that appears while a trade is already open - which on a trending day is most of them.")
preset = input.string("Balanced", "Preset",
     options = ["Sniper (fewest, best measured)", "Balanced", "Scalper (most signals)", "Manual"],
     group = gP,
     tooltip = "This is a genuine trade-off, not a free lunch. Sniper uses the gates that MEASURED best (ADX<=20, ATR<=1.0x median) and will sit out most of the day. Scalper opens the gates wide and WILL take setups that measured worse. Balanced sits between. Manual uses your own values below.")

gF = "EXPANSION FILTER  (the 'is this move going to be big?' test)"
gateAll = input.bool(true, "Apply this filter to EVERY setup type", group = gF,
     tooltip = "OFF is the honest default. The filter was measured on BREAK setups - sweeps of liquidity - and that is the only place the evidence covers. Applying it to retests, pullbacks and breakouts as well would throttle them on the strength of a result that was never about them. Turn it on if you want fewer, more selective signals across the board.")
useSqueeze = input.bool(true, "Only trade compressed volatility", group = gF,
     tooltip = "MEASURED: ATR at or below its median precedes 3+ ATR moves ~90% of the time. ATR at 2x its median precedes them 25% of the time.")
maxSqueeze = input.float(1.15, "   max ATR / median ATR", minval = 0.5, step = 0.05, group = gF)
useAdx     = input.bool(true, "Only trade below an ADX ceiling", group = gF)
maxAdx     = input.float(25,  "   max ADX", minval = 10, maxval = 60, step = 1, group = gF)
useSess    = input.bool(false, "Restrict to hours (UTC)", group = gF,
     tooltip = "On GOLD 15m: 04-12 UTC showed 89-93% chance of a 3+ ATR move; 14-18 UTC showed 62-72%. Off by default because it was measured on one instrument.")
sessFrom   = input.int(4,  "   from hour", minval = 0, maxval = 23, group = gF)
sessTo     = input.int(12, "   to hour",   minval = 0, maxval = 23, group = gF)

gR = "RISK"
slBuf     = input.float(0.30, "Stop beyond sweep extreme (× ATR)", minval = 0, step = 0.05, group = gR)
tpMode    = input.string("Next liquidity", "Target", options = ["Next liquidity", "Fixed R"], group = gR)
tpR       = input.float(2.0, "   fixed R multiple", minval = 0.5, step = 0.25, group = gR)
minR      = input.float(1.5, "Reject setup if R:R below", minval = 0.5, step = 0.1, group = gR,
     tooltip = "Lowered from 1.2, and the reason is measured rather than a preference: the 'needs room to run' idea was tested on its own across 8 markets and came out NEGATIVE in 5 of them (-0.097R median). Demanding a big R:R before entry was costing setups without buying quality, so the floor is lower and you judge the trade on the order book's R:R column instead.")
maxRiskAtr= input.float(2.5, "Reject setup if stop wider than (× ATR)", minval = 0.5, step = 0.1, group = gR)

gT = "LIVE TRADING (manual)"
lotSize    = input.float(0.01, "Your lot size", minval = 0.01, step = 0.01, group = gT,
     tooltip = "Used only to convert points into money on the panel. Change it and every P/L figure rescales.")
ccyPerPt   = input.float(1.00, "Money per 1.0 of price, at 0.01 lots", minval = 0.01, step = 0.01, group = gT,
     tooltip = "XAUUSD at 0.01 lots is normally 1.00 of account currency per 1.00 of price (a 100 oz contract at 0.01 = 1 oz). Check your broker's contract size and adjust if it differs.")
maxChase   = input.float(0.50, "MISSED IT if price already ran this far ahead", minval = 0.1, step = 0.1, group = gT,
     tooltip = "THE SLOW-CHART PROTECTION. TradingView can be late, the alert can arrive after the move, you can be looking away. If price has already travelled this much of your risk in the signal's direction before you are filled, it is no longer the trade you were shown - same stop, less reward. It is marked MISSED instead of taken.")
entryDelay = input.int(1, "How many bars until I actually click", minval = 1, maxval = 10, group = gT,
     tooltip = "THE HONESTY SETTING. A signal prints at a bar close; you see it, then you enter. Set this to how many bars that realistically takes you. Everything tracked - entry price, P/L, the whole record - uses THAT price, not the signal price. At 0 the numbers flatter you.")
trackFrom  = input.string("My real entry", "Measure profit from", options = ["My real entry", "Signal price"], group = gT)
showHist   = input.int(5, "How many finished trades to keep on the chart", minval = 0, maxval = 20, group = gT,
     tooltip = "Past position boxes stay on the chart, drained of colour, so you can see how the last N setups actually played out instead of only the live one. The record table below shows up to 11 of them.")
showTable  = input.bool(true, "Show my results table", group = gT)

gE = "CLOSING EARLY  (not every trade reaches TP)"
useTrailExit = input.bool(true, "Close when the ATR trail is hit", group = gE,
     tooltip = "The trail only arms once the trade is in profit, and once armed it only ever moves in your favour. Hitting it closes the trade for whatever it is worth at that moment - which is usually less than TP and much more than nothing. Marked TRL in the record.")
useTimeExit  = input.bool(true, "Close a trade that stalls", group = gE,
     tooltip = "A setup that has not resolved in this many bars has stopped being the setup you entered. Closing it frees the slot for one that is working. Marked TIME.")
holdMins2    = input.int(600, "   stalled after (MINUTES)", minval = 10, maxval = 20000, group = gE,
     tooltip = "600 minutes is 10 hours - the 40 bars intended on M15. On M1, 40 bars is 40 minutes, and E-040 measured that a one-minute hold on gold has only about twice the spread to work with. Cutting a trade off that early at M1 is a mechanical way to lose.")
maxHoldRaw2  = input.int(40, "   or a fixed bar count (auto off)", minval = 5, maxval = 2000, group = gE)
maxHoldBars  = autoTf ? math.max(5, math.round(holdMins2 / barMins)) : maxHoldRaw2
useFlipExit  = input.bool(false, "Close on an opposite signal", group = gE,
     tooltip = "If a fresh signal fires the other way, the reason you are in the trade has gone. Off by default: on a choppy chart this closes good trades early. Marked FLIP.")

gV = "CLOSE ON A REVERSAL"
useRevExit = input.bool(true, "Close early if the trend turns against me", group = gV,
     tooltip = "You asked for this: take the profit when a reversal shows up rather than riding it back to the stop. A trade is closed the moment the signal that justified it flips the other way. Marked REV.")
revMinR    = input.float(0.0, "   only when the trade is this far in profit (R)", step = 0.1, group = gV,
     tooltip = "0 closes on any reversal while not losing. Set it NEGATIVE (e.g. -0.5) to also cut a losing trade early when the trend turns - that stops you paying the full stop on a trade that is already wrong.")

gD = "DISPLAY"
showTrail = input.bool(true, "Show trail guide once trade is in profit", group = gD,
     tooltip = "Dashed line showing where an ATR trail would sit. It deliberately does NOT appear until the trade is in profit: moving a stop up early measured as the worst exit rule on all four markets tested.")
trailArm  = input.float(1.0, "   only after (R in profit)", minval = 0, step = 0.25, group = gD)
trailAtr  = input.float(2.0, "   trail distance (× ATR)", minval = 0.5, step = 0.25, group = gD)
showRlive = input.bool(true, "Show live R on the open trade", group = gD)
zoneGrow  = input.bool(true, "Liquidity zones extend to the right edge", group = gD)
theme     = input.string("White / blue candles", "Palette",
     options = ["White / blue candles", "Classic green / red", "Custom"], group = gD,
     tooltip = "Zones are deliberately faint so white and blue candles stay the brightest thing on the chart. Only the BUY/SELL labels are fully saturated.")
uBull     = input.color(color.new(#29B6F6, 0),  "Custom buy",  inline = "c", group = gD)
uBear     = input.color(color.new(#FF5370, 0),  "Custom sell", inline = "c", group = gD)

// One accent per side, then everything else is a transparency of that accent.
// Keeping a single hue per side is what stops the chart turning into confetti.
accBull = theme == "Classic green / red" ? #26A69A : theme == "Custom" ? uBull : #29B6F6
accBear = theme == "Classic green / red" ? #EF5350 : theme == "Custom" ? uBear : #FF5370

cBull   = accBull
cBear   = accBear
cBsl    = color.new(accBear, 86)   // sell-side liquidity sits ABOVE price
cSsl    = color.new(accBull, 86)   // buy-side liquidity sits BELOW price
// Fully transparent. Drawings are created unconditionally and hidden with
// this, never by putting a *.new() call inside a ternary.
HIDDEN  = color.new(color.gray, 100)
cTp     = color.new(accBull, 88)
cSl     = color.new(accBear, 88)
showPanel = input.bool(true, "Show status panel", group = gD)
cleanMode = input.bool(true, "CLEAN MODE - levels, signals and the order book only",
     group = gD,
     tooltip = "Hides the zone-record labels, the setup-name labels, the trend line and the two history tables. What is left is the levels, the buy/sell marks, the position boxes, the panel and the order book. Use it when you want to read price rather than read the indicator.")
showWhy   = input.bool(true, "Label each signal with which setup it was", group = gD)
showEma   = input.bool(true, "Draw the trend line", group = gD)

// ──────────────────────────────── FOUNDATIONS ───────────────────────────────
atr    = ta.atr(14)
atrMed = ta.median(atr, 50)
[diP, diM, adxV] = ta.dmi(14, 14)
sqz    = atrMed > 0 ? atr / atrMed : 1.0

// bars are read one late so a forming bar can never revoke a confirmed pivot
ph = ta.pivothigh(high, pivLen, pivLen)[1]
pl = ta.pivotlow(low,  pivLen, pivLen)[1]
pivotBar = bar_index - 1 - pivLen

// Two shorter scales. Each is read [1] and confirmed its own length late, so
// the shorter ones are known SOONER — which is the point: a scalper's level
// exists long before an 8-bar pivot confirms it.
ph2 = useMulti ? ta.pivothigh(high, pivMid, pivMid)[1] : na
pl2 = useMulti ? ta.pivotlow(low,  pivMid, pivMid)[1] : na
ph3 = useMulti ? ta.pivothigh(high, pivFast, pivFast)[1] : na
pl3 = useMulti ? ta.pivotlow(low,  pivFast, pivFast)[1] : na
pivotBar2 = bar_index - 1 - pivMid
pivotBar3 = bar_index - 1 - pivFast

hourNow = hour(time, "UTC")
inSess  = not useSess or (sessFrom <= sessTo ? (hourNow >= sessFrom and hourNow < sessTo)
                                             : (hourNow >= sessFrom or hourNow < sessTo))
// Preset overrides the manual gates unless "Manual" is chosen.
// The numbers come from the measured table: ADX 0-15 preceded a 3+ ATR move
// ~90% of the time, ADX>=40 only 66%, ATR at 2x its median only 25%.
sqzGate = preset == "Sniper (fewest, best measured)" ? 1.00 :
          preset == "Balanced" ? 1.15 :
          preset == "Scalper (most signals)" ? 1.60 : maxSqueeze
adxGate = preset == "Sniper (fewest, best measured)" ? 20.0 :
          preset == "Balanced" ? 25.0 :
          preset == "Scalper (most signals)" ? 45.0 : maxAdx

expansionOk = (not useSqueeze or sqz <= sqzGate) and (not useAdx or adxV <= adxGate) and inSess

// ---- previous-day and session levels.
// The [1] IS required. lookahead_off stops the call leaking the FUTURE; it does
// not step back a day. Without the offset this returns the DEVELOPING daily
// bar - today's running high - so on a trending day it produced a fresh level
// at every new high, five minutes later those became eligible, and price
// closing above them manufactured a stream of "break" longs against a line
// drawn at the current high. The earlier comment here asserted the opposite and
// was wrong.
pdh = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "D", high[1], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)
pdl = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "D", low[1], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off)

// rolling session extremes, reset when the session block changes
sessId  = math.floor(hour(time, "UTC") / 8)          // 0 Asia, 1 London, 2 NY
newSess = sessId != sessId[1]
var float sHi = na
var float sLo = na
if newSess
    sHi := high
    sLo := low
else
    sHi := math.max(nz(sHi, high), high)
    sLo := math.min(nz(sLo, low), low)

// ───────────────────────────── LIQUIDITY REGISTRY ───────────────────────────
// Parallel arrays: price, bar formed, touch count, swept flag, drawing handles.
var array<float> hiPx   = array.new_float()
var array<int>   hiBar  = array.new_int()
var array<int>   hiHits = array.new_int()
var array<bool>  hiDead = array.new_bool()
var array<line>  hiLn   = array.new_line()
var array<box>   hiBx   = array.new_box()
var array<bool>  hiStk  = array.new_bool()

var array<float> loPx   = array.new_float()
var array<int>   loBar  = array.new_int()
var array<int>   loHits = array.new_int()
var array<bool>  loDead = array.new_bool()
var array<line>  loLn   = array.new_line()
var array<box>   loBx   = array.new_box()
var array<bool>  loStk  = array.new_bool()

// ───────────────── ZONE MEMORY — how this level behaved before ──────────────
// The question: when price comes back to a zone, what did it DO the last times
// it was here? This memory is deliberately SEPARATE from the drawn levels.
// Drawings get evicted to keep the chart readable, but the history of a price
// area should outlive its line - that is the "previous zones" part.
var array<float> memPx    = array.new_float()
var array<int>   memTouch = array.new_int()
var array<int>   memHold  = array.new_int()
var array<int>   memBreak = array.new_int()
var array<float> memMove  = array.new_float()
var array<int>   memLast  = array.new_int()
// Side matters. Resistance at 3400 and support at 3400 are different
// behaviours, and without this they shared one record - so "5 held" could be
// the sum of holds from above and holds from below, which is not a fact about
// anything. A level that breaks and flips is the most common event on a chart,
// so this was not an edge case.
var array<bool>  memHi    = array.new_bool()

// touches whose reaction is not yet known
var array<float> pnPx  = array.new_float()
var array<int>   pnBar = array.new_int()
var array<bool>  pnHi  = array.new_bool()

memFind(float p, bool isHigh) =>
    idx = -1
    tol = zoneTol * atr
    if array.size(memPx) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(memPx) - 1
            if idx == -1 and array.get(memHi, i) == isHigh
                 and math.abs(array.get(memPx, i) - p) <= tol
                idx := i
    idx

memAdd(float p, bool isHigh) =>
    array.push(memHi, isHigh)
    array.push(memPx, p)
    array.push(memTouch, 0)
    array.push(memHold, 0)
    array.push(memBreak, 0)
    array.push(memMove, 0.0)
    array.push(memLast, bar_index)
    while array.size(memPx) > memMax
        array.shift(memHi)
        array.shift(memPx)
        array.shift(memTouch)
        array.shift(memHold)
        array.shift(memBreak)
        array.shift(memMove)
        array.shift(memLast)
    array.size(memPx) - 1

regTouch(float p, bool isHigh) =>
    i = memFind(p, isHigh)
    if i == -1
        i := memAdd(p, isHigh)
    array.set(memTouch, i, array.get(memTouch, i) + 1)
    array.set(memLast,  i, bar_index)
    array.push(pnPx,  p)
    array.push(pnBar, bar_index)
    array.push(pnHi,  isHigh)
    0

// Record a visit to any live level. Re-registering the same zone every bar of
// a long consolidation would turn one visit into forty, so a zone is only
// re-registered once its previous visit has been resolved.
touchScan(array<float> px, array<bool> dead, bool isHigh) =>
    if useZoneMem and barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(px) > 0
        tol = zoneTol * atr
        for i = 0 to array.size(px) - 1
            if not array.get(dead, i)
                p = array.get(px, i)
                if low <= p + tol and high >= p - tol
                    m = memFind(p, isHigh)
                    if m == -1 or (bar_index - array.get(memLast, m)) > reactBars
                        regTouch(p, isHigh)
    0

addLevel(array<float> px, array<int> bar, array<int> hits, array<bool> dead,
         array<line> lns, array<box> bxs, array<bool> stk,
         float p, int b, bool isHigh, bool sticky) =>
    bool merged = false
    // merge into an existing near-equal level: equal highs/lows hold MORE
    // resting orders than a lone extreme, so they are strengthened not doubled
    // A level on the WRONG SIDE of price - a pivot high already below us,
    // because higher-timeframe and weekly extremes arrive late - is still a
    // real level worth SEEING. Broken resistance becomes support; that is half
    // of structure trading. What it must not do is arm a sweep: it would be
    // "broken" the moment it is registered, manufacturing a signal against a
    // level price cleared an hour ago.
    //
    // So it is registered and drawn like any other, and marked dead on arrival.
    // Dead levels are excluded from the sweep loops but still show on the chart
    // and still work as retest and flip zones. Not drawing them at all was an
    // over-correction that removed levels Veer wants to see.
    bornDead = (isHigh and p <= close) or (not isHigh and p >= close)

    tol = eqTol * atr
    if array.size(px) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(px) - 1
            if not merged and not array.get(dead, i) and math.abs(array.get(px, i) - p) <= tol
                array.set(px, i, isHigh ? math.max(array.get(px, i), p) : math.min(array.get(px, i), p))
                array.set(hits, i, array.get(hits, i) + 1)
                merged := true
    if not merged
        array.push(px, p)
        array.push(bar, b)
        array.push(hits, 1)
        array.push(dead, bornDead)
        array.push(stk, sticky)
        ln = line.new(b, p, bar_index + 3, p, style = line.style_dotted, width = 1,
             color = showLvls ? (isHigh ? cBsl : cSsl) : HIDDEN)
        bx = box.new(b, p, bar_index + 3, p, border_color = na,
             bgcolor = showZones ? (isHigh ? cBsl : cSsl) : HIDDEN)
        array.push(lns, ln)
        array.push(bxs, bx)
        // Age out first. lvlMaxAge was declared and never referenced, so the
        // ONLY eviction was the FIFO cap below - and on M1 a 2-bar pivot
        // arrives every few minutes, so the previous-day and session levels
        // were pushed out within about 100 bars while micro-pivots filled the
        // array. Retiring by age keeps the structural levels alive.
        if array.size(px) > 0
            for j = array.size(px) - 1 to 0
                // sticky levels are exempt: a weekly high is registered at the
                // bar we LEARN it, so ageing it out on an M1 chart would
                // discard it after a few hours - the exact opposite of the
                // point of keeping levels that reach back days.
                if not array.get(stk, j) and bar_index - array.get(bar, j) > lvlMaxAge
                    l1 = array.get(lns, j)
                    b1 = array.get(bxs, j)
                    if not na(l1)
                        line.delete(l1)
                    if not na(b1)
                        box.delete(b1)
                    array.remove(px, j)
                    array.remove(bar, j)
                    array.remove(hits, j)
                    array.remove(dead, j)
                    array.remove(lns, j)
                    array.remove(bxs, j)
                    array.remove(stk, j)

        // Evict past the cap - but drop the oldest ORDINARY level first. A
        // daily, weekly or higher-timeframe level is marked sticky and is only
        // discarded when nothing else is left, so the M1 pivot churn cannot
        // push the levels that matter off the chart.
        while array.size(px) > maxLevels
            victim = 0
            if stickyKeep
                victim := -1
                for j = 0 to array.size(px) - 1
                    if victim == -1 and not array.get(stk, j)
                        victim := j
                if victim == -1
                    victim := 0        // all sticky: fall back to the oldest
            l0 = array.get(lns, victim)
            b0 = array.get(bxs, victim)
            if not na(l0)
                line.delete(l0)
            if not na(b0)
                box.delete(b0)
            array.remove(px, victim)
            array.remove(bar, victim)
            array.remove(hits, victim)
            array.remove(dead, victim)
            array.remove(lns, victim)
            array.remove(bxs, victim)
            array.remove(stk, victim)
    merged

if not na(ph)
    addLevel(hiPx, hiBar, hiHits, hiDead, hiLn, hiBx, hiStk, ph, pivotBar, true, false)
if not na(pl)
    addLevel(loPx, loBar, loHits, loDead, loLn, loBx, loStk, pl, pivotBar, false, false)
if not na(ph2)
    addLevel(hiPx, hiBar, hiHits, hiDead, hiLn, hiBx, hiStk, ph2, pivotBar2, true, false)
if not na(pl2)
    addLevel(loPx, loBar, loHits, loDead, loLn, loBx, loStk, pl2, pivotBar2, false, false)
if not na(ph3)
    addLevel(hiPx, hiBar, hiHits, hiDead, hiLn, hiBx, hiStk, ph3, pivotBar3, true, false)
if not na(pl3)
    addLevel(loPx, loBar, loHits, loDead, loLn, loBx, loStk, pl3, pivotBar3, false, false)

// previous day — registered once per new value, not once per bar
var float lastPdh = na
var float lastPdl = na
if useDaily and not na(pdh) and pdh != lastPdh
    lastPdh := pdh
    addLevel(hiPx, hiBar, hiHits, hiDead, hiLn, hiBx, hiStk, pdh, bar_index, true, true)
if useDaily and not na(pdl) and pdl != lastPdl
    lastPdl := pdl
    addLevel(loPx, loBar, loHits, loDead, loLn, loBx, loStk, pdl, bar_index, false, true)

// session extremes — registered when the session rolls over
if useSessH and newSess and not na(sHi[1])
    addLevel(hiPx, hiBar, hiHits, hiDead, hiLn, hiBx, hiStk, sHi[1], bar_index, true, true)
if useSessH and newSess and not na(sLo[1])
    addLevel(loPx, loBar, loHits, loDead, loLn, loBx, loStk, sLo[1], bar_index, false, true)

// ─────────────── LEVELS FROM HIGHER TIMEFRAMES AND FURTHER BACK ─────────────
// Read from the higher timeframe directly, so an M1 chart still carries the
// M15/H1/H4 swings and last week's extremes. The [1] on the pivot plus
// lookahead_off means each value is only delivered once its HTF bar has
// closed - nothing here can repaint.
//
// A timeframe LOWER than the chart is skipped rather than requested: asking a
// 4H chart for M15 pivots returns values the chart cannot represent, and would
// silently litter it with levels that mean nothing at that scale. This is what
// makes the same settings usable on M1 and on H4.
htfOk(string tf) =>
    useHtf and timeframe.in_seconds(tf) > timeframe.in_seconds(timeframe.period)

htfHi(string tf) =>
    htfOk(tf) ? request.security(syminfo.tickerid, tf,
         ta.pivothigh(high, htfPiv, htfPiv)[1], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off) : na
htfLo(string tf) =>
    htfOk(tf) ? request.security(syminfo.tickerid, tf,
         ta.pivotlow(low, htfPiv, htfPiv)[1], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off) : na

addHtf(float hp, float lp) =>
    if not na(hp)
        addLevel(hiPx, hiBar, hiHits, hiDead, hiLn, hiBx, hiStk, hp, bar_index, true, true)
    if not na(lp)
        addLevel(loPx, loBar, loHits, loDead, loLn, loBx, loStk, lp, bar_index, false, true)
    0

h1h = htfHi(htf1)
h1l = htfLo(htf1)
h2h = htfHi(htf2)
h2l = htfLo(htf2)
h3h = htfHi(htf3)
h3l = htfLo(htf3)

// Register only when the value CHANGES. Without this every bar would re-add
// the same level, and addLevel's merge would just keep bumping its hit count,
// making a stale level look like the most retested price on the chart.
var float p1h = na
var float p1l = na
var float p2h = na
var float p2l = na
var float p3h = na
var float p3l = na
if h1h != p1h or h1l != p1l
    p1h := h1h
    p1l := h1l
    addHtf(h1h, h1l)
if h2h != p2h or h2l != p2l
    p2h := h2h
    p2l := h2l
    addHtf(h2h, h2l)
if h3h != p3h or h3l != p3l
    p3h := h3h
    p3l := h3l
    addHtf(h3h, h3l)

// previous WEEK high and low - the levels price still respects days later
pwh = useWeek ? request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "W", high[1], lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off) : na
pwl = useWeek ? request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "W", low[1],  lookahead = barmerge.lookahead_off) : na
var float lastPwh = na
if not na(pwh) and pwh != lastPwh
    lastPwh := pwh
    addHtf(pwh, pwl)

// round numbers near price
var float lastRound = na
if useRound and roundStep > 0
    rUp = math.ceil(close / roundStep) * roundStep
    rDn = math.floor(close / roundStep) * roundStep
    if rUp != lastRound
        lastRound := rUp
        addLevel(hiPx, hiBar, hiHits, hiDead, hiLn, hiBx, hiStk, rUp, bar_index, true, false)
        addLevel(loPx, loBar, loHits, loDead, loLn, loBx, loStk, rDn, bar_index, false, false)

// keep live level drawings anchored to the right edge, fade the dead ones
extend(array<float> px, array<int> bar, array<int> hits, array<bool> dead,
       array<line> lns, array<box> bxs, bool isHigh) =>
    if array.size(px) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(px) - 1
            ln = array.get(lns, i)
            bx = array.get(bxs, i)
            p  = array.get(px, i)
            isDead = array.get(dead, i)
            thick = math.min(array.get(hits, i), 3)
            if not na(ln)
                // y must be re-set too: an equal-high merge MOVES the stored
                // price, and without this the line drifts away from its own
                // zone and the chart looks broken.
                line.set_y1(ln, p)
                line.set_y2(ln, p)
                line.set_x2(ln, bar_index + 3)
                line.set_color(ln, not showLvls ? HIDDEN
                     : isDead ? color.new(color.gray, 88)
                     : color.new(isHigh ? accBear : accBull, 45))
                line.set_width(ln, isDead ? 1 : thick)
            if not na(bx)
                zh = (zoneGrow ? 0.16 : 0.07) * atr * thick
                box.set_top(bx, p + (isHigh ? zh : 0))
                box.set_bottom(bx, p - (isHigh ? 0 : zh))
                box.set_right(bx, zoneGrow ? bar_index + 6 : bar_index + 3)
                box.set_bgcolor(bx, not showZones ? HIDDEN
                     : isDead ? color.new(color.gray, 95)
                     : (isHigh ? cBsl : cSsl))
    0
extend(hiPx, hiBar, hiHits, hiDead, hiLn, hiBx, true)
extend(loPx, loBar, loHits, loDead, loLn, loBx, false)

// ---- record visits, then judge them `reactBars` later
touchScan(hiPx, hiDead, true)
touchScan(loPx, loDead, false)

if array.size(pnPx) > 0
    for q = array.size(pnPx) - 1 to 0
        if bar_index - array.get(pnBar, q) >= reactBars
            lp   = array.get(pnPx, q)
            isHi = array.get(pnHi, q)
            m    = memFind(lp, isHi)
            if m != -1
                thr   = zoneTol * atr
                moved = close - lp
                // a resistance HELD if price is now below it; a support HELD if
                // price is now above it. Anything inside the tolerance is
                // neither - it is still sitting on the level, so it is not counted.
                held  = isHi ? (moved < -thr) : (moved >  thr)
                broke = isHi ? (moved >  thr) : (moved < -thr)
                if held
                    array.set(memHold, m, array.get(memHold, m) + 1)
                    array.set(memMove, m, array.get(memMove, m) + math.abs(moved))
                else if broke
                    array.set(memBreak, m, array.get(memBreak, m) + 1)
                    array.set(memMove, m, array.get(memMove, m) + math.abs(moved))
            array.remove(pnPx,  q)
            array.remove(pnBar, q)
            array.remove(pnHi,  q)

// ---- what the nearest zone's record says about what price may do here
zoneHold(float p, bool isHigh) =>
    m = memFind(p, isHigh)
    m == -1 ? -1.0 : (array.get(memHold, m) + array.get(memBreak, m) == 0 ? -1.0
         : 100.0 * array.get(memHold, m)
           / (array.get(memHold, m) + array.get(memBreak, m)))

zoneText(float p, bool isHigh) =>
    m = memFind(p, isHigh)
    string t = ""
    if m != -1
        h = array.get(memHold, m)
        b = array.get(memBreak, m)
        n = h + b
        avg = n > 0 ? array.get(memMove, m) / n : 0.0
        t := n == 0 ? "new"
             : str.tostring(h) + " held / " + str.tostring(b) + " broke  ~"
               + str.tostring(avg, "#.#") + "pt"
    t

// Sweep marks are permanent labels, one per sweep. On M1 that is hundreds in a
// session, which buries the chart and eats the 500-label budget until
// TradingView starts deleting other things instead. Only the recent ones stay.
//
// Defined HERE, above the sweep loops that call it. Pine is single-pass: a
// function must appear before its first use, and having this further down the
// file is what produced "Could not find function or function reference".
var array<label> swLabs = array.new_label()

keepSweep(label l) =>
    array.push(swLabs, l)
    while array.size(swLabs) > 25
        l0 = array.shift(swLabs)
        if not na(l0)
            label.delete(l0)
    0

// ───────────────────────────── SWEEP DETECTION ──────────────────────────────
// A break is: price takes out a level that is old enough to hold orders, and
// (optionally) CLOSES beyond it on an expanding candle. The close requirement
// is what removes most fakeouts — a wick through is not a break.
body     = math.abs(close - open)
rng      = math.max(high - low, syminfo.mintick)
isDisp   = body >= dispAtr * atr
dispOk   = not needDisp or isDisp

float sweptHi = na
float sweptLo = na
int   sweptHiHits = 0
int   sweptLoHits = 0

if array.size(hiPx) > 0
    for i = 0 to array.size(hiPx) - 1
        p = array.get(hiPx, i)
        if not array.get(hiDead, i) and bar_index - array.get(hiBar, i) >= minLvlAge
            broke = needClose ? (close > p) : (high > p)
            if broke and barstate.isconfirmed
                array.set(hiDead, i, true)
                // The grab, marked where it happened. A level going grey is
                // easy to miss; this is the event the whole strategy is built
                // on and it should be impossible to miss.
                if showSweeps
                    keepSweep(label.new(bar_index, p, "SWEEP", size = size.tiny,
                         style = label.style_label_down,
                         color = color.new(color.white, 100),
                         textcolor = color.new(accBear, 15)))
                if na(sweptHi) or p > sweptHi
                    sweptHi := p
                    sweptHiHits := array.get(hiHits, i)

if array.size(loPx) > 0
    for i = 0 to array.size(loPx) - 1
        p = array.get(loPx, i)
        if not array.get(loDead, i) and bar_index - array.get(loBar, i) >= minLvlAge
            broke = needClose ? (close < p) : (low < p)
            if broke and barstate.isconfirmed
                array.set(loDead, i, true)
                if showSweeps
                    keepSweep(label.new(bar_index, p, "SWEEP", size = size.tiny,
                         style = label.style_label_up,
                         color = color.new(color.white, 100),
                         textcolor = color.new(accBull, 15)))
                if na(sweptLo) or p < sweptLo
                    sweptLo := p
                    sweptLoHits := array.get(loHits, i)

// ---- BOUNCE: price TESTS a level and holds it.
// This is the setup class that was missing. A break is one thing that can
// happen at a level; holding is the other, and at support/resistance it is
// the more common one. Ignoring it was the largest source of missed entries.
rngB  = math.max(high - low, syminfo.mintick)
upWk  = high - math.max(open, close)
dnWk  = math.min(open, close) - low
tolB  = bounceTol * atr

float bounceUpLvl = na       // held support -> buy
float bounceDnLvl = na       // held resistance -> sell

if useBounce and barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(loPx) > 0
    for i = 0 to array.size(loPx) - 1
        p = array.get(loPx, i)
        if not array.get(loDead, i) and bar_index - array.get(loBar, i) >= minLvlAge
            tested = low <= p + tolB and low >= p - tolB * 3
            held   = close > p
            reject = (dnWk / rngB) >= bounceWick
            if tested and held and reject and (na(bounceUpLvl) or p > bounceUpLvl)
                bounceUpLvl := p

if useBounce and barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(hiPx) > 0
    for i = 0 to array.size(hiPx) - 1
        p = array.get(hiPx, i)
        if not array.get(hiDead, i) and bar_index - array.get(hiBar, i) >= minLvlAge
            tested = high >= p - tolB and high <= p + tolB * 3
            held   = close < p
            reject = (upWk / rngB) >= bounceWick
            if tested and held and reject and (na(bounceDnLvl) or p < bounceDnLvl)
                bounceDnLvl := p

// ════════════════════ MARKET STRUCTURE ═══════════════════════════════════════
// The context everything else sits inside. Higher highs AND higher lows is an
// uptrend. The moment price takes out the last higher LOW instead of the last
// high, the trend has changed character - that is a CHoCH, and it is the
// earliest honest warning that the side you are trading has stopped working.
var float swHi  = na       // most recent confirmed swing high
var float swHiP = na       // the one before it
var float swLo  = na
var float swLoP = na
var int   bias  = 0        // +1 bullish structure, -1 bearish, 0 undecided

if not na(ph)
    swHiP := swHi
    swHi  := ph
if not na(pl)
    swLoP := swLo
    swLo  := pl

// A break of STRUCTURE is a close beyond the last swing, not a wick through it.
bosUp   = useStruct and not na(swHi) and barstate.isconfirmed and close > swHi and close[1] <= swHi
bosDn   = useStruct and not na(swLo) and barstate.isconfirmed and close < swLo and close[1] >= swLo
chochUp = bosUp and bias <= 0      // was bearish or undecided, now broke up
chochDn = bosDn and bias >= 0

var int lastBosBar = 0
if bosUp
    bias := 1
    lastBosBar := bar_index
if bosDn
    bias := -1
    lastBosBar := bar_index
// A break of structure two days ago is not a description of today. Without a
// decay the Structure row stayed green through days of range, and with the
// bias filter on it kept refusing every short on the strength of it.
if bias != 0 and bar_index - lastBosBar > biasLife
    bias := 0

structTxt = bias > 0 ? "BULLISH" : bias < 0 ? "BEARISH" : "undecided"

// ---- PREMIUM / DISCOUNT. Cheap or expensive against the recent swing. On a
// day that has opened very low this is what says price is in discount, rather
// than it being a guess.
rHi  = ta.highest(high, rangeLen)
rLo  = ta.lowest(low, rangeLen)
rMid = (rHi + rLo) / 2
inDiscount = useDiscount and close < rMid
inPremium  = useDiscount and close > rMid
rangePos   = rHi > rLo ? (close - rLo) / (rHi - rLo) * 100.0 : 50.0

// ════════════════════ FAIR VALUE GAPS ════════════════════════════════════════
// Three bars where the middle one moves so fast the first bar's high never
// meets the third bar's low. Price fills those far more often than not, and the
// edge of the gap is a precise entry rather than a rough zone.
fvgUp   = useFvg and barstate.isconfirmed and low > high[2]
         and (low - high[2]) >= fvgMin * atr
fvgDn   = useFvg and barstate.isconfirmed and high < low[2]
         and (low[2] - high) >= fvgMin * atr

var array<float> fvTop = array.new_float()
var array<float> fvBot = array.new_float()
var array<int>   fvDir = array.new_int()
var array<box>   fvBx  = array.new_box()
var array<bool>  fvInv = array.new_bool()   // has this gap FAILED and flipped?

addFvg(float top, float bot, int dir) =>
    array.push(fvInv, false)
    array.push(fvTop, top)
    array.push(fvBot, bot)
    array.push(fvDir, dir)
    array.push(fvBx, box.new(bar_index - 2, top, bar_index + 8, bot, border_color = na,
         bgcolor = showSmc ? color.new(dir > 0 ? accBull : accBear, 90) : HIDDEN))
    while array.size(fvTop) > 12
        b0 = array.shift(fvBx)
        if not na(b0)
            box.delete(b0)
        array.shift(fvInv)
        array.shift(fvTop)
        array.shift(fvBot)
        array.shift(fvDir)
    0

if fvgUp
    addFvg(low, high[2], 1)
if fvgDn
    addFvg(low[2], high, -1)

// An unfilled gap that price has come back into is the entry - ONCE. Without
// removing it, the same gap re-fires on every bar price spends inside it, which
// on M1 is dozens of identical signals from one imbalance.
//
// INVERSION comes first: a gap that price CLOSES clean through has failed. The
// side defending it was wrong, and the zone now works from the other side. It
// is flipped rather than deleted, because an inverted gap has already proved
// which side lost, which is more information than the original gap carried.
float fvLong  = na
float fvShort = na
var bool lastWasInv = false
if useIfvg and array.size(fvTop) > 0
    for i = array.size(fvTop) - 1 to 0
        t = array.get(fvTop, i)
        b = array.get(fvBot, i)
        dr = array.get(fvDir, i)
        if barstate.isconfirmed and not array.get(fvInv, i)
            failed = dr > 0 ? close < b : close > t
            if failed
                array.set(fvDir, i, -dr)
                array.set(fvInv, i, true)
                bxi = array.get(fvBx, i)
                if not na(bxi)
                    box.set_bgcolor(bxi, color.new(dr > 0 ? accBear : accBull, 82))
                    box.set_border_color(bxi, color.new(dr > 0 ? accBear : accBull, 40))

if array.size(fvTop) > 0
    for i = array.size(fvTop) - 1 to 0
        t  = array.get(fvTop, i)
        b  = array.get(fvBot, i)
        dr = array.get(fvDir, i)
        if barstate.isconfirmed and low <= t and high >= b
            took = false
            if dr > 0 and close > b and na(fvLong)
                fvLong := b
                took := true
            if dr < 0 and close < t and na(fvShort)
                fvShort := t
                took := true
            if took
                lastWasInv := array.get(fvInv, i)
                // DELETE it. Greying it and dropping the handle leaked the box:
                // unreachable, never reclaimed, and on M1 gold enough gaps form
                // to reach max_boxes_count within a session - at which point
                // TradingView evicts the OLDEST boxes, which are the liquidity
                // zones, while extend() keeps writing to their dead handles.
                bx0 = array.get(fvBx, i)
                if not na(bx0)
                    box.delete(bx0)
                array.remove(fvTop, i)
                array.remove(fvBot, i)
                array.remove(fvDir, i)
                array.remove(fvInv, i)
                array.remove(fvBx, i)

// ════════════════════ ORDER BLOCKS ═══════════════════════════════════════════
// The last DOWN candle before a move that breaks structure upward is where the
// buying actually came from. Returning there is the tightest honest retest on
// the chart.
var float obUpTop = na
var float obUpBot = na
var float obDnTop = na
var float obDnBot = na
var int   obUpBar = 0
var int   obDnBar = 0

var box obUpBox = na
var box obDnBox = na

if useOb and bosUp
    // walk back for the most recent bearish candle before this impulse
    for j = 1 to 20
        if close[j] < open[j]
            obUpTop := high[j]
            obUpBot := low[j]
            obUpBar := bar_index
            if showSmc
                if not na(obUpBox)
                    box.delete(obUpBox)
                obUpBox := box.new(bar_index - j, high[j], bar_index + 10, low[j],
                     border_color = color.new(accBull, 55),
                     bgcolor = color.new(accBull, 85))
            break
if useOb and bosDn
    for j = 1 to 20
        if close[j] > open[j]
            obDnTop := high[j]
            obDnBot := low[j]
            obDnBar := bar_index
            if showSmc
                if not na(obDnBox)
                    box.delete(obDnBox)
                obDnBox := box.new(bar_index - j, high[j], bar_index + 10, low[j],
                     border_color = color.new(accBear, 55),
                     bgcolor = color.new(accBear, 85))
            break

// A return INTO the block, not merely near it: the bar must trade inside the
// block's range and close back out on the correct side. Testing only
// `low <= obUpTop` fired on essentially every bar once price was anywhere
// below the block's high. An order block also goes stale - price that has not
// come back within obLife bars is not coming back to that one.
obUpLive = useOb and not na(obUpBot) and (bar_index - obUpBar) <= obLife
obDnLive = useOb and not na(obDnTop) and (bar_index - obDnBar) <= obLife
obLong  = obUpLive and barstate.isconfirmed
          and low <= obUpTop and low >= obUpBot - zoneTol * atr and close > obUpBot
obShort = obDnLive and barstate.isconfirmed
          and high >= obDnBot and high <= obDnTop + zoneTol * atr and close < obDnTop

// The level must be CAPTURED before the block is cleared, or setupLvl below
// reads na and the stop falls back to the signal bar's close.
float obLvlL = obLong  ? obUpBot : na
float obLvlS = obShort ? obDnTop : na

// Blocks are spent where the trade is actually QUEUED, further down - not
// here, where the setup has merely been detected and may still be refused.

// ─────────────────────── RETEST / RECLAIM SETUPS ────────────────────────────
// A level that has been taken out is not finished - it is the next level, from
// the other side. Old resistance becomes support. Previously this only existed
// as a GATE on break entries; as a setup of its own it fires far more often and
// gives a much tighter stop than chasing the break candle.
var array<float> rcPx  = array.new_float()
var array<int>   rcBar = array.new_int()
var array<int>   rcDir = array.new_int()      // +1 broken upward, -1 downward

pushReclaim(float p, int dir) =>
    array.push(rcPx, p)
    array.push(rcBar, bar_index)
    array.push(rcDir, dir)
    while array.size(rcPx) > 40
        array.shift(rcPx)
        array.shift(rcBar)
        array.shift(rcDir)
    0

if not na(sweptHi)
    pushReclaim(sweptHi, 1)
if not na(sweptLo)
    pushReclaim(sweptLo, -1)

float rcLong  = na
float rcShort = na
if useReclaim and barstate.isconfirmed and array.size(rcPx) > 0
    tolR = bounceTol * atr
    for i = array.size(rcPx) - 1 to 0
        p   = array.get(rcPx, i)
        age = bar_index - array.get(rcBar, i)
        dr  = array.get(rcDir, i)
        if age >= 1 and age <= reclaimWin
            // came back to it and held on the side it broke to
            back = low <= p + tolR and high >= p - tolR
            if back and dr > 0 and close > p and na(rcLong)
                rcLong := p
            if back and dr < 0 and close < p and na(rcShort)
                rcShort := p

// ─────────────────────── TREND PULLBACK SETUPS ──────────────────────────────
// In a clean trend price does not return to a pivot - it returns to the moving
// average and goes again. Without this, most continuation entries in a trend
// are never offered at all.
emaT   = ta.ema(close, emaLen)
emaUp  = emaT > emaT[2]
emaDn  = emaT < emaT[2]
tolE   = emaTol * atr
pbLong  = usePullEma and barstate.isconfirmed and emaUp
          and low <= emaT + tolE and close > emaT and close > open
pbShort = usePullEma and barstate.isconfirmed and emaDn
          and high >= emaT - tolE and close < emaT and close < open

// ─────────────────────────── BREAKOUT SETUPS ────────────────────────────────
// A quiet range that ends. This fires where the level-based setups have
// nothing to say, which is most of a consolidation.
boxHi   = ta.highest(high, boxLen)[1]
boxLo   = ta.lowest(low, boxLen)[1]
boxWide = boxHi - boxLo
tightOk = boxWide > 0 and boxWide <= boxTight * atr
boLong  = useBreakout and barstate.isconfirmed and tightOk and close > boxHi
boShort = useBreakout and barstate.isconfirmed and tightOk and close < boxLo

// direction: continuation follows the break, reversal fades it
isCont   = mode == "Continuation (tested)"
rawLong  = not na(sweptHi)      // took out sell-side liquidity above
rawShort = not na(sweptLo)      // took out buy-side liquidity below
brkLong  = useBreak and (isCont ? rawLong  : rawShort)
brkShort = useBreak and (isCont ? rawShort : rawLong)

// A bounce off support is a BUY regardless of the continuation/reversal
// setting — that setting governs how a BREAK is traded, not a hold.
bncLong  = useBounce and not na(bounceUpLvl)
bncShort = useBounce and not na(bounceDnLvl)

// ---- ZONE HISTORY FILTER.
// A bounce is a bet that the level HOLDS, so take it where this zone has held
// more often than it broke. A break is the opposite bet, so take it where the
// zone has actually been broken before. A zone with no record (-1) is allowed
// through either way - absence of history is not evidence against the trade.
// A setup is a BOUNCE when only the bounce side fired. Declared here, above
// the two helpers that read it, because Pine is single-pass.
// Everything except a raw break invalidates at the SIGNAL BAR's extreme, which
// is a much tighter stop than the swept extreme a break needs. Grouping them
// here keeps the stop rule in one place instead of four.
newLong  = not na(rcLong)  or pbLong  or boLong  or not na(fvLong)  or obLong
newShort = not na(rcShort) or pbShort or boShort or not na(fvShort) or obShort
setupIsBounce = (bncLong or bncShort or newLong or newShort)
                 and not (brkLong or brkShort)

// THE LEVEL A SETUP IS ABOUT. A continuation long comes from a swept HIGH; a
// reversal long comes from a swept LOW; a bounce long comes from the support
// that held. Assuming "long implies sweptHi" was only true in continuation
// mode, and na everywhere else - which fell back to `close`, i.e. no level.
setupLvl(int dir) =>
    float lv = na
    if dir > 0
        lv := not na(fvLong) ? fvLong : obLong ? obLvlL
              : not na(rcLong) ? rcLong : bncLong ? bounceUpLvl
              : pbLong ? emaT : boLong ? boxHi
              : (isCont ? sweptHi : sweptLo)
    else
        lv := not na(fvShort) ? fvShort : obShort ? obLvlS
              : not na(rcShort) ? rcShort : bncShort ? bounceDnLvl
              : pbShort ? emaT : boShort ? boxLo
              : (isCont ? sweptLo : sweptHi)
    lv

// Which SIDE of the book the setup's level lives on: a long bounces off a
// support (a low), a short bounces off a resistance (a high); a continuation
// long breaks a high, a reversal long fades a broken low.
setupIsHigh(int dir) =>
    setupIsBounce ? (dir < 0) : (isCont ? (dir > 0) : (dir < 0))

bncHold  = bncLong  ? zoneHold(bounceUpLvl, false)
           : bncShort ? zoneHold(bounceDnLvl, true) : -1.0
brkHold  = brkLong  ? zoneHold(nz(setupLvl(1), close),  setupIsHigh(1))
           : brkShort ? zoneHold(nz(setupLvl(-1), close), setupIsHigh(-1)) : -1.0
bncOk    = not useZoneFilter or bncHold < 0 or bncHold >= 50.0
brkOk    = not useZoneFilter or brkHold < 0 or brkHold <  50.0

// A single spike can take out levels on BOTH sides - an ordinary news minute
// on M1. Both brkLong and brkShort then fire, and `dir = goLong ? 1 : -1`
// silently resolved that to LONG every time, printing a BUY on a bar that
// closed down. The bar's own close breaks the tie: it is the only evidence on
// that bar about which side actually gave way.
// Trading WITH structure is a different bet from trading against it. This is
// the switch that refuses the counter-trend ones - the trades that turn into
// the big losses - at the cost of every early reversal entry.
biasLongOk  = not biasFilter or bias >= 0
biasShortOk = not biasFilter or bias <= 0

anyLong  = ((brkLong  and brkOk) or (bncLong  and bncOk) or newLong)  and biasLongOk
anyShort = ((brkShort and brkOk) or (bncShort and bncOk) or newShort) and biasShortOk

bothWays = anyLong and anyShort
tieLong  = close >= open

goLong   = anyLong  and (not bothWays or tieLong)
goShort  = anyShort and (not bothWays or not tieLong)

// Which setup it was, so the chart says WHY it fired rather than just that it
// did. Read in priority order, most specific first.
// Named PER DIRECTION and in the SAME priority order setupLvl uses. The old
// version ORed the two sides, so a BUY could be labelled "GAP FILL" from a
// bearish gap on the other side of price, and its priority was OB-before-FVG
// while setupLvl used FVG-before-OB - the label named one zone while the stop
// was anchored to a different one.
nameFor(int dir) =>
    dir > 0
         ? (not na(fvLong) ? (lastWasInv ? "INVERSE GAP" : "GAP FILL")
            : obLong ? "ORDER BLOCK"
            : not na(rcLong) ? "RETEST" : pbLong ? "PULLBACK"
            : boLong ? "BREAKOUT" : bncLong ? "BOUNCE" : "BREAK")
         : (not na(fvShort) ? (lastWasInv ? "INVERSE GAP" : "GAP FILL")
            : obShort ? "ORDER BLOCK"
            : not na(rcShort) ? "RETEST" : pbShort ? "PULLBACK"
            : boShort ? "BREAKOUT" : bncShort ? "BOUNCE" : "BREAK")

setupName = goLong ? nameFor(1) : goShort ? nameFor(-1) : "BREAK"

// ─────────────────────────── TARGET: NEXT LIQUIDITY ─────────────────────────
nextLiq(bool up) =>
    float best = na
    if up and array.size(hiPx) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(hiPx) - 1
            p = array.get(hiPx, i)
            if not array.get(hiDead, i) and p > close
                best := na(best) ? p : math.min(best, p)
    if not up and array.size(loPx) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(loPx) - 1
            p = array.get(loPx, i)
            if not array.get(loDead, i) and p < close
                best := na(best) ? p : math.max(best, p)
    best

// ───────────────────────────── TRADE STATE + BOXES ──────────────────────────
// ───────────────────────── TRADE STATE (multiple concurrent) ────────────────
// Parallel arrays so several setups can run at once. With only one slot the
// indicator ignores every setup that appears while a trade is already open,
// which on a trending day is most of them — that single limit was the largest
// cause of "it does not catch enough entries".
var array<int>   trDir = array.new_int()
var array<float> trEnt = array.new_float()
var array<float> trSl  = array.new_float()
var array<float> trTp  = array.new_float()
var array<int>   trBar = array.new_int()
var array<box>   trBxT = array.new_box()
var array<box>   trBxS = array.new_box()
var array<line>  trTrl = array.new_line()
var array<line>  trEnl = array.new_line()   // the line from your real fill
var array<float> trTrs = array.new_float()  // ratcheting trail stop, per trade
var array<float> trMfe = array.new_float()  // best price the trade ever saw
var array<string> trSet = array.new_string()  // which setup type produced it
var array<box>   oldBoxes = array.new_box()
var array<line>  oldLines = array.new_line()

// ---- PENDING QUEUE.
// A signal prints at a bar close. You see it, then you enter. Those are not
// the same price and pretending they are is how a chart flatters itself.
// A fired signal sits here for `entryDelay` bars and is then filled at that
// bar's OPEN — the first price actually obtainable.
var array<int>   pdDir  = array.new_int()
var array<int>   pdBar  = array.new_int()
var array<float> pdRisk = array.new_float()
var array<float> pdRR   = array.new_float()
var array<float> pdSig  = array.new_float()   // the signal price, for comparison
var array<string> pdSet = array.new_string()  // which setup type produced it

// ---- completed trade record, newest last
var array<int>   hDir = array.new_int()
var array<float> hEnt = array.new_float()
var array<float> hExt = array.new_float()
var array<float> hR   = array.new_float()
var array<float>  hMfe = array.new_float()   // the best it was EVER worth, in R
var array<float>  hRsk = array.new_float()   // risk in price, to price the gap
var array<string> hSet = array.new_string()   // which setup type produced it
var array<string> hWhy = array.new_string()

// Completed trades do not vanish. Their boxes stay, drained of colour, so the
// last `showHist` trades read as history rather than competing with the live one.
pushOld(box b, bool won) =>
    if not na(b)
        box.set_bgcolor(b, color.new(won ? accBull : accBear, 93))
        array.push(oldBoxes, b)
        while array.size(oldBoxes) > math.max(showHist, 1) * 2
            b0 = array.shift(oldBoxes)
            if not na(b0)
                box.delete(b0)
    0

pushOldLine(line l) =>
    if not na(l)
        line.set_color(l, color.new(color.gray, 55))
        line.set_width(l, 1)
        array.push(oldLines, l)
        while array.size(oldLines) > math.max(showHist, 1)
            l0 = array.shift(oldLines)
            if not na(l0)
                line.delete(l0)
    0

// One place where a trade is opened, so a delayed fill and an immediate fill
// cannot drift apart.
openTrade(int d, float en, float sl, float tp, string setup) =>
    array.push(trSet, setup)
    array.push(trDir, d)
    array.push(trEnt, en)
    array.push(trSl,  sl)
    array.push(trTp,  tp)
    array.push(trBar, bar_index)
    array.push(trTrs, float(na))
    array.push(trMfe, 0.0)
    array.push(trTrl, line(na))
    // Borders, not just a wash of colour: the edge of the target box IS the
    // take profit and the edge of the stop box IS the stop, so they should be
    // lines you can read a price off rather than a fade into the background.
    array.push(trBxT, box.new(bar_index, math.max(en, tp), bar_index + 2,
         math.min(en, tp), bgcolor = color.new(accBull, 85),
         border_color = color.new(accBull, 30), border_width = 1))
    array.push(trBxS, box.new(bar_index, math.max(en, sl), bar_index + 2,
         math.min(en, sl), bgcolor = color.new(accBear, 85),
         border_color = color.new(accBear, 30), border_width = 1))
    // the line the user asked for: drawn at the price actually obtainable,
    // and extended every bar so the trade is trackable from entry to close
    array.push(trEnl, line.new(bar_index, en, bar_index + 2, en, width = 2,
         color = color.new(d > 0 ? accBull : accBear, 10)))
    0

// ---- armed state (only used when needRetest is on)
var int   armDir  = 0
var float armLvl  = na
var int   armBar  = 0
var float armExt  = na

// Displacement is a BREAK requirement. Demanding an expansion candle on a
// bounce would reject the very thing a bounce is: a rejection wick.
// A break must expand; a bounce is a rejection wick and demanding expansion on
// it would reject the very thing it is. The expansion gate applies to breaks
// unless you explicitly extend it to everything.
isBreakSetup = (brkLong or brkShort) and not setupIsBounce
gateOk   = gateAll ? expansionOk : (isBreakSetup ? expansionOk : true)
breakNow = barstate.isconfirmed and gateOk and (goLong or goShort) and
     (isBreakSetup ? dispOk : true)

if needRetest and breakNow
    armDir := goLong ? 1 : -1
    // The retest must be OF THE LEVEL, not of the signal bar's close. With
    // bounces on (default) or reversal mode, sweptHi/sweptLo are na, so this
    // armed at `close` and then waited for price to return to an arbitrary
    // price with no line on it - silently breaking the very option the
    // tooltip tells you to switch on when you are being faked out.
    armLvl := nz(setupLvl(goLong ? 1 : -1), close)
    armBar := bar_index
    armExt := goLong ? high : low
if armDir != 0
    armExt := armDir > 0 ? math.max(armExt, high) : math.min(armExt, low)
    if bar_index - armBar > retestBars
        armDir := 0

// A retest fires when price returns to the broken level and CLOSES on the
// correct side. Coming back and failing to hold is the fakeout; this refuses it.
retestHit = armDir != 0 and bar_index > armBar and barstate.isconfirmed and
     (armDir > 0 ? (low <= armLvl and close > armLvl)
                 : (high >= armLvl and close < armLvl))

// THE CAP MUST COUNT WHAT IS ALREADY QUEUED. Counting only OPEN trades, while
// canFire is evaluated BEFORE the fill loop, meant bar 1 queued, bar 2 filled
// it and queued another while still seeing zero open, and so on. A setup that
// stayed true for four bars - an EMA pullback, a level that keeps
// re-qualifying - became three live positions a few points apart, three stops
// under the same swing, and a 1R loss turned into 3R.
//
// The cooldown is the other half: a condition true on consecutive bars is ONE
// idea, not one per bar.
// Declared HERE, above canFire, because the cap has to be readable at the
// moment a signal is considered - not where the panel happens to display it.
var int nSigDay  = 0
var int nSigAll  = 0
var int dayMark  = 0
if dayofmonth != dayMark
    dayMark := dayofmonth
    nSigDay := 0

var int lastFireBar = -1000
cooledOk = bar_index - lastFireBar >= entryCool
canFire = array.size(trDir) + array.size(pdDir) < maxTrades and cooledOk
     and nSigDay < maxPerDay
     and (needRetest ? retestHit : breakNow)

var bool firedLong   = false
var bool firedShort  = false
var bool firedBounce = false
firedLong   := false
firedShort  := false
firedBounce := false

if canFire
    dir   = needRetest ? armDir : (goLong ? 1 : -1)
    // a bounce is invalidated if the level FAILS, so its stop sits just beyond
    // the wick that tested it — normally much tighter than a break stop
    // The stop sits beyond the extreme that would INVALIDATE the setup.
    // Previously `ext` was a max-of-highs for a long and was then consumed by
    // math.min(low, ext) - and since ext >= high >= low, that is always just
    // `low`. The sweep extreme and the retest extreme could never move the
    // stop at all, in any branch, despite the input being named for them.
    ext   = setupIsBounce ? (dir > 0 ? low : high)
             : needRetest ? armExt
             : (dir > 0 ? math.min(low,  nz(sweptLo, low))
                        : math.max(high, nz(sweptHi, high)))
    entry = close
    sl    = dir > 0 ? math.min(low, ext) - slBuf * atr : math.max(high, ext) + slBuf * atr
    risk  = math.abs(entry - sl)
    lvlTp = nextLiq(dir > 0)
    tp    = tpMode == "Fixed R" or na(lvlTp) ? entry + dir * tpR * risk : lvlTp
    rr    = risk > 0 ? math.abs(tp - entry) / risk : 0
    if risk > 0 and risk <= maxRiskAtr * atr and rr >= minR
        // The signal is real now; the FILL is not. Queue it and let the fill
        // happen at a price this bar cannot know. The minimum delay is 1 bar
        // and that is not a preference: at 0 the fill used this bar's OPEN, a
        // price that occurred BEFORE the bar closed and before the signal
        // existed, and the same bar's high and low then resolved the target.
        // Signal bars are displacement or rejection candles by construction,
        // so that manufactured same-bar wins. It was look-ahead, not optimism.
        array.push(pdDir,  dir)
        array.push(pdBar,  bar_index)
        array.push(pdRisk, risk)
        array.push(pdRR,   rr)
        array.push(pdSig,  entry)
        array.push(pdSet,  setupName)
        lastFireBar := bar_index
        firedLong   := dir > 0
        firedShort  := dir < 0
        firedBounce := setupIsBounce
        armDir := 0

        // ---- D-3: a zone is spent only when a trade ACTUALLY queues.
        // These used to be cleared where the condition was evaluated, so a bar
        // refused by the expansion gate, the both-ways tie-break, the risk cap
        // or the R:R floor still destroyed the zone - the best zone on the
        // chart went quiet after a bar that did nothing.
        if obLong
            obUpTop := na
            obUpBot := na
        if obShort
            obDnTop := na
            obDnBot := na

// ---- fill anything whose delay has elapsed, at THIS bar's open
var float lastSlip = na
var float lastMiss = na
// A miss only matters while it is RECENT. A running total since the chart
// loaded says nothing about whether you are late today.
var array<int> missBars = array.new_int()
if array.size(pdDir) > 0
    for q = array.size(pdDir) - 1 to 0
        aged = bar_index - array.get(pdBar, q)
        d2   = array.get(pdDir,  q)
        rk   = array.get(pdRisk, q)
        rrq  = array.get(pdRR,   q)
        sig  = array.get(pdSig,  q)

        // how far the market has already gone your way before you got on. If
        // it has run more than maxChase of the risk, this is no longer the
        // trade you were shown - same stop, less reward - so it is recorded as
        // MISSED rather than quietly taken at the worse price.
        chase   = (open - sig) * d2
        tooLate = chase > maxChase * rk

        if aged >= entryDelay and array.size(trDir) < maxTrades and not tooLate
            // THE realistic price: this bar's open, the first thing you could
            // actually have been filled at after seeing the signal.
            fill = trackFrom == "Signal price" ? sig : open
            sl2  = fill - d2 * rk
            tp2  = fill + d2 * rrq * rk
            lastSlip := (sig - fill) * d2      // + means the delay cost you
            openTrade(d2, fill, sl2, tp2, array.get(pdSet, q))
            array.remove(pdDir, q)
            array.remove(pdBar, q)
            array.remove(pdRisk, q)
            array.remove(pdRR, q)
            array.remove(pdSig, q)
            array.remove(pdSet, q)
        else if (tooLate and aged >= entryDelay) or aged > entryDelay + 3
            array.push(missBars, bar_index)
            lastMiss := chase
            array.remove(pdDir, q)
            array.remove(pdBar, q)
            array.remove(pdRisk, q)
            array.remove(pdRR, q)
            array.remove(pdSig, q)
            array.remove(pdSet, q)

// ---- manage every open trade. Iterating backwards so removals are safe.
// TODAY'S MONEY - where you actually stand right now, which the results table
// (a rolling window of trades) does not tell you.
var float dayPnl = 0.0
var int   dayMk  = 0
if dayofmonth != dayMk
    dayMk  := dayofmonth
    dayPnl := 0.0

if array.size(missBars) > 0
    while array.size(missBars) > 0 and bar_index - array.get(missBars, 0) > 200
        array.shift(missBars)
nMissed = array.size(missBars)

var string lastExit = ""
if array.size(trDir) > 0
    for k = array.size(trDir) - 1 to 0
        d  = array.get(trDir, k)
        en = array.get(trEnt, k)
        sl = array.get(trSl,  k)
        tp = array.get(trTp,  k)
        bt = array.get(trBxT, k)
        bs = array.get(trBxS, k)
        tl = array.get(trTrl, k)
        el = array.get(trEnl, k)
        held = bar_index - array.get(trBar, k)

        // boxes and the entry line GROW with the trade instead of sitting at a
        // fixed width, so an open position is trackable from fill to close
        box.set_right(bt, bar_index + 2)
        box.set_right(bs, bar_index + 2)
        if not na(el)
            line.set_x2(el, bar_index + 2)

        risk = math.abs(en - sl)
        rNow = risk > 0 ? (close - en) * d / risk : 0

        // ---- EXITS ARE TESTED AGAINST THE TRAIL AS IT STOOD BEFORE THIS BAR.
        // The trail is derived from a bar's CLOSE. Testing it against that same
        // bar's LOW asks whether a price was breached by a level that did not
        // exist yet when the low printed - and it fires constantly on exactly
        // the displacement bars this strategy enters on. The ratchet therefore
        // runs AFTER the exit test, below.
        trs = array.get(trTrs, k)

        useTrl = useTrailExit and not na(trs)
        eStop  = useTrl ? (d > 0 ? math.max(sl, trs) : math.min(sl, trs)) : sl
        byTrl  = useTrl and (d > 0 ? trs > sl : trs < sl)
        hitStp = d > 0 ? low  <= eStop : high >= eStop
        hitTp  = d > 0 ? high >= tp    : low  <= tp
        // held > 0 for the same reason as hitRev: the fire block and the fill
        // loop both run BEFORE this one, so without it an opposite signal on
        // the fill bar closed the trade on the bar it opened.
        hitFlp = useFlipExit and held > 0 and (d > 0 ? firedShort : firedLong)
        hitTim = useTimeExit and held >= maxHoldBars
        // ---- REVERSAL. Price closing back through the level the setup was
        // built on means the reason for the trade is gone. Riding that back to
        // the stop is the give-back, and it is avoidable.
        // held > 0: the fill loop runs BEFORE this one, so a trade opened on
        // this bar is already here and would otherwise be closed on its own
        // entry bar - a one-bar box and an exit no one could have taken.
        revBar = d > 0 ? (close < open and close < low[1]) : (close > open and close > high[1])
        hitRev = useRevExit and held > 0 and rNow >= revMinR and revBar

        // ---- A GAP IS FILLED AT THE GAP, NOT AT THE STOP. If the bar OPENS
        // beyond the stop, the broker fills there. Booking it at the stop
        // understates precisely the losses that matter, and one-sidedly: a gap
        // through the target is still booked at the target.
        stopFill = d > 0 ? math.min(eStop, open) : math.max(eStop, open)
        exPx  = hitStp ? stopFill : hitTp ? tp : close
        exWhy = hitStp ? (byTrl ? "TRL" : "SL") : hitTp ? "TP"
                 : hitFlp ? "FLIP" : hitRev ? "REV" : "TIME"
        gone  = hitStp or hitTp or hitFlp or hitRev or hitTim

        // ---- PEAK, capped at the exit on the bar the trade dies. Without the
        // cap, a long stopped out at a bar's low that then rallied was credited
        // with the whole rally - money it could never have taken, because it
        // was already flat - and that fiction was priced and shown as "left
        // behind".
        favBar = ((d > 0 ? high : low) - en) * d
        favNow = gone ? math.min(favBar, (exPx - en) * d) : favBar
        mfe    = math.max(array.get(trMfe, k), favNow)
        array.set(trMfe, k, mfe)
        peakR  = risk > 0 ? mfe / risk : 0.0

        // ---- TRAIL RATCHET, after the exit test. Arms only once the trade has
        // EARNED it (early break-even measured worst on all four markets), and
        // once armed it never gives ground back.
        if rNow >= trailArm and not gone
            cand = d > 0 ? close - trailAtr * atr : close + trailAtr * atr
            trs := na(trs) ? cand : (d > 0 ? math.max(trs, cand) : math.min(trs, cand))
            array.set(trTrs, k, trs)

        if showTrail and not na(trs)
            if na(tl)
                tl := line.new(bar_index - 1, trs, bar_index + 2, trs,
                     color = color.new(d > 0 ? accBull : accBear, 40),
                     style = line.style_dashed, width = 1)
                array.set(trTrl, k, tl)
            else
                line.set_xy1(tl, bar_index - 1, trs)
                line.set_xy2(tl, bar_index + 2, trs)

        // live money at YOUR lot size, not abstract R
        moneyNow = (close - en) * d * ccyPerPt * (lotSize / 0.01)
        if showRlive and barstate.islast
            label.new(bar_index + 2, close,
                 str.tostring(rNow, "#.#") + "R  " +
                 (moneyNow >= 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(moneyNow, "#.##"),
                 style = label.style_label_left,
                 color = color.new(color.white, 100),
                 textcolor = moneyNow >= 0 ? accBull : accBear, size = size.small)

        if barstate.isconfirmed and gone
            lastExit := exWhy
            rFinal = risk > 0 ? (exPx - en) * d / risk : 0.0
            won    = rFinal >= 0
            // money on the mark itself, so every past position shows what it
            // made without opening the table
            exMoney = (exPx - en) * d * ccyPerPt * (lotSize / 0.01)
            dayPnl += exMoney
            // The money used to ride on every exit mark, and at a hundred
            // trades a day that is a hundred price-tagged labels over the
            // candles. The mark says WHAT happened; the record table says how
            // much. Only a genuinely large result is worth the ink.
            bigOne = math.abs(rFinal) >= 2.0
            label.new(bar_index, exPx,
                 (exWhy == "TP" ? "✓" : exWhy == "SL" ? "✕" : exWhy)
                 + (bigOne ? "  " + (exMoney >= 0 ? "+" : "")
                    + str.tostring(exMoney, "#.##") : ""),
                 style = d > 0 ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down,
                 color = color.new(color.white, 100),
                 textcolor = won ? accBull : accBear, size = size.small)
            array.push(hDir, d)
            array.push(hEnt, en)
            array.push(hExt, exPx)
            array.push(hR,   rFinal)
            array.push(hMfe, peakR)
            array.push(hRsk, risk)
            array.push(hSet, array.get(trSet, k))
            array.push(hWhy, exWhy)
            // 500, not 30. E-046 computed what the scoreboard actually costs:
            // 53 completed trades PER SETUP TYPE to detect a catastrophically
            // bad one. At 30 total across eight types that is under 4 each, so
            // the scoreboard could never answer anything at any trade rate.
            // 500 gives roughly 62 per type - enough for the one question that
            // is reachable. Pine handles arrays this size without difficulty;
            // the old number was a guess, not a constraint.
            while array.size(hDir) > 500
                array.shift(hDir)
                array.shift(hEnt)
                array.shift(hExt)
                array.shift(hR)
                array.shift(hMfe)
                array.shift(hRsk)
                array.shift(hSet)
                array.shift(hWhy)
            pushOld(bt, won)
            pushOld(bs, won)
            pushOldLine(el)
            if not na(tl)
                line.delete(tl)
            array.remove(trDir, k)
            array.remove(trEnt, k)
            array.remove(trSl,  k)
            array.remove(trTp,  k)
            array.remove(trBar, k)
            array.remove(trBxT, k)
            array.remove(trBxS, k)
            array.remove(trTrl, k)
            array.remove(trEnl, k)
            array.remove(trTrs, k)
            array.remove(trMfe, k)
            array.remove(trSet, k)

// EVERY signal gets the full BUY/SELL marker. Previously `setupIsBounce` was
// true for order block, gap fill, retest, pullback and breakout - five of the
// seven types - which routed them all to a tiny unlabelled triangle while the
// alert for the same bar said "BUY". The setup-name label below says which
// type it was, so the triangle carried no information anyway.
plotshape(firedLong,  "BUY",  shape.labelup,
     location.belowbar, cBull, text = "BUY",  textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)
plotshape(firedShort, "SELL", shape.labeldown,
     location.abovebar, cBear, text = "SELL", textcolor = color.white, size = size.small)

// WHY it fired, not just that it did. Knowing which setup type is paying and
// which is not is the only way to decide what to switch off.
if (firedLong or firedShort) and showWhy and not cleanMode
    label.new(bar_index, firedLong ? low : high, setupName,
         style = firedLong ? label.style_label_up : label.style_label_down,
         color = color.new(color.white, 100), size = size.tiny,
         textcolor = color.new(firedLong ? accBull : accBear, 25))
// Bounce setups get their own mark. They are a different trade with a
// different invalidation, and blending them into one label would hide which
// kind is actually working for you.
plot(showEma and usePullEma and not cleanMode ? emaT : na, "Trend line", color.new(color.gray, 30), 1)

// CHoCH was computed and only ever shown in a panel cell guarded by
// barstate.islast, so it was visible for one tick and then gone forever. It is
// the earliest warning the side you are trading has stopped working, which
// makes it worth a mark on the bar it happened.
plotshape(chochUp,  "CHoCH up",   shape.diamond, location.belowbar,
     color.new(accBull, 20), size = size.tiny)
plotshape(chochDn,  "CHoCH down", shape.diamond, location.abovebar,
     color.new(accBear, 20), size = size.tiny)

// ---- HOW OFTEN IS THIS ACTUALLY FIRING?
// Whether the settings are too tight is not judgeable from one screen. This
// counts what fired so the question has an answer.
var float tFirst = na
if na(tFirst)
    tFirst := time
if firedLong or firedShort
    nSigAll += 1
    nSigDay += 1
// calendar span, so weekends are counted and this reads slightly LOW rather
// than flattering the number
spanDays = (time - tFirst) / 86400000.0
perDay   = spanDays > 0.5 ? nSigAll / spanDays : na



// ──────────────── ZONE RECORD LABELS — the history, on the chart ────────────
// Drawn only on the last bar so they cost one label per level, not one per bar.
var array<label> zLabs = array.new_label()

// A level that has been touched more than once is an EQUAL HIGH or EQUAL LOW -
// the strongest liquidity there is, because every one of those touches left
// stops behind it. The merge logic already counts them; it just never said so.
zoneName(bool isHigh, int hits) =>
    hits >= 2 ? (isHigh ? "EQH x" + str.tostring(hits) : "EQL x" + str.tostring(hits))
              : (isHigh ? "SSL" : "BSL")

drawZoneLabels(array<float> px, array<bool> dead, array<int> hits, bool isHigh) =>
    if showZoneStats and not cleanMode and useZoneMem and barstate.islast and array.size(px) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(px) - 1
            if not array.get(dead, i) and math.abs(array.get(px, i) - close) <= labelNear * atr
                p = array.get(px, i)
                t = zoneText(p, isHigh)
                // BSL / SSL by name. Sell-side liquidity is the resting stops
                // ABOVE price (shorts' stops, which a rally runs into); buy-side
                // is the stops BELOW. Naming them removes the guesswork of
                // reading it off the colour.
                tag = zoneName(isHigh, array.get(hits, i)) + (t == "" ? "" : "  " + t)
                if true
                    hp = zoneHold(p, isHigh)
                    col = hp < 0 ? color.new(chart.fg_color, 45)
                         : hp >= 60 ? accBull : hp <= 40 ? accBear
                         : color.new(#FFB300, 0)
                    array.push(zLabs, label.new(bar_index + 4, p, tag,
                         style = label.style_label_left, size = size.tiny,
                         color = color.new(color.white, 100), textcolor = col))
    0

// labels are redrawn each time the last bar updates, so clear the previous set
if barstate.islast and array.size(zLabs) > 0
    while array.size(zLabs) > 0
        l0 = array.shift(zLabs)
        if not na(l0)
            label.delete(l0)
drawZoneLabels(hiPx, hiDead, hiHits, true)
drawZoneLabels(loPx, loDead, loHits, false)

// ──────────────────────────────── STATUS PANEL ──────────────────────────────
var table panel = table.new(position.top_right, 2, 14, frame_width = 1,
     frame_color = color.new(color.gray, 60), border_width = 1,
     border_color = color.new(color.gray, 85))

cell(int r, string k, string v, color c) =>
    table.cell(panel, 0, r, k, text_size = size.small, text_halign = text.align_left,
         text_color = color.new(chart.fg_color, 40), bgcolor = color.new(chart.bg_color, 10))
    table.cell(panel, 1, r, v, text_size = size.small, text_halign = text.align_right,
         text_color = c, bgcolor = color.new(chart.bg_color, 10))

okc = accBull
bad = accBear
dim = color.new(chart.fg_color, 35)

if showPanel and barstate.islast
    // The money first, then the context a structure trader reads before
    // deciding anything: which way structure is pointing, and whether price is
    // cheap or expensive inside its own range.
    openM = 0.0
    if array.size(trDir) > 0
        for k2 = 0 to array.size(trDir) - 1
            openM += (close - array.get(trEnt, k2)) * array.get(trDir, k2)
                     * ccyPerPt * (lotSize / 0.01)
    totToday = dayPnl + openM
    cell(0, "TODAY  at " + str.tostring(lotSize) + " lots",
         (totToday >= 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(totToday, "#.##")
         + (openM != 0 ? "   (" + (openM >= 0 ? "+" : "")
            + str.tostring(openM, "#.##") + " open)" : ""),
         totToday > 0 ? okc : totToday < 0 ? bad : dim)
    cell(1, "Structure", structTxt
         + (chochUp ? "  CHoCH up" : chochDn ? "  CHoCH down" : ""),
         bias > 0 ? okc : bias < 0 ? bad : dim)
    cell(2, "Price is", (inDiscount ? "CHEAP - discount" : inPremium ? "expensive - premium" : "mid")
         + "   " + str.tostring(rangePos, "#") + "% of range",
         inDiscount ? okc : inPremium ? bad : dim)
    cell(3, "Mode", (isCont ? "CONT" : "REV") + " · " +
         (preset == "Sniper (fewest, best measured)" ? "SNIPER"
        : preset == "Scalper (most signals)" ? "SCALPER"
        : preset == "Balanced" ? "BALANCED" : "MANUAL"), isCont ? okc : bad)
    cell(4, "Squeeze  ATR/med", str.tostring(sqz, "#.##") + " / " + str.tostring(sqzGate, "#.##")
         + (sqz <= sqzGate ? "  ok" : "  too hot"), sqz <= sqzGate ? okc : bad)
    cell(5, "ADX", str.tostring(adxV, "#.#") + " / " + str.tostring(adxGate, "#.#")
         + (adxV <= adxGate ? "  ok" : "  too high"), adxV <= adxGate ? okc : bad)
    cell(6, "Session " + str.tostring(hourNow) + ":00 UTC", inSess ? "ok" : "outside", inSess ? okc : bad)
    // count every enabled type, not just the two that existed first
    nOn = (useBreak ? 1 : 0) + (useBounce ? 1 : 0) + (useReclaim ? 1 : 0)
         + (usePullEma ? 1 : 0) + (useBreakout ? 1 : 0) + (useFvg ? 1 : 0)
         + (useOb ? 1 : 0)
    cell(7, "Setups on", str.tostring(nOn) + " of 7", nOn > 0 ? okc : bad)
    // The expansion gate applies to BREAK setups only unless gateAll is on.
    // Reporting it as global printed "NO - waiting" in red on bars that were
    // firing order-block and pullback entries.
    cell(8, gateAll ? "All setups allowed" : "Breaks allowed",
         expansionOk ? "YES" : (gateAll ? "NO — waiting" : "no (others still on)"),
         expansionOk ? okc : gateAll ? bad : dim)
    nOpen = array.size(trDir)
    nPend = array.size(pdDir)
    cell(9, "Trades", nOpen > 0 ? (str.tostring(nOpen) + "/" + str.tostring(maxTrades) + " open")
         : nPend > 0 ? ("ENTER NOW × " + str.tostring(nPend))
         : armDir != 0 ? ("ARMED " + str.tostring(bar_index - armBar) + "/" + str.tostring(retestBars))
         : ("flat" + (lastExit == "" ? "" : "  last " + lastExit)),
         nOpen > 0 ? okc : nPend > 0 ? color.new(#FFB300, 0)
         : armDir != 0 ? color.new(#FFB300, 0) : color.new(chart.fg_color, 30))
    // What the delay between signal and click actually cost, in price. This is
    // the number most indicators hide by pretending you filled at the signal.
    // The nearest zone's own record, so you know what price did here before.
    nearHi = nextLiq(true)
    nearLo = nextLiq(false)
    pickHi = na(nearLo) or (not na(nearHi)
             and math.abs(nearHi - close) < math.abs(close - nearLo))
    nearP  = pickHi ? nearHi : nearLo
    zt = na(nearP) ? "" : zoneText(nearP, pickHi)
    zh = na(nearP) ? -1.0 : zoneHold(nearP, pickHi)
    cell(10, "Nearest zone " + (na(nearP) ? "" : str.tostring(nearP, format.mintick)),
         zt == "" ? "no record yet" : zt,
         zh < 0 ? dim : zh >= 60 ? okc : zh <= 40 ? bad : color.new(#FFB300, 0))
    // Whether it is firing often enough is a question with an answer, and one
    // screen is not it.
    cell(13, "Signals", str.tostring(nSigDay) + " today"
         + (na(perDay) ? "" : "   " + str.tostring(perDay, "#.#") + "/day avg"),
         nSigDay > 0 ? okc : dim)
    cell(11, "MISSED lately", nMissed == 0 ? "none"
         : str.tostring(nMissed) + (na(lastMiss) ? ""
           : "   last ran " + str.tostring(lastMiss, "#.##")),
         nMissed == 0 ? okc : color.new(#FFB300, 0))
    cell(12, "Delay " + str.tostring(entryDelay) + " bar" + (entryDelay == 1 ? "" : "s"),
         na(lastSlip) ? "no fills yet"
         : ((lastSlip >= 0 ? "cost " : "gained ") + str.tostring(math.abs(lastSlip), "#.##")),
         na(lastSlip) ? color.new(chart.fg_color, 30) : lastSlip > 0 ? bad : okc)

// ───────────────────────────── TRADE RECORD TABLE ───────────────────────────
// What actually happened, in money, at your lot size. Every figure uses the
// DELAYED fill price, so this is the record of trades you could really have
// taken - not the record the signal price would have produced.
var table rec = table.new(position.bottom_right, 6, 15, frame_width = 1,
     frame_color = color.new(color.gray, 60), border_width = 1,
     border_color = color.new(color.gray, 85))

recCell(int c, int r, string txt, color col, bool head) =>
    table.cell(rec, c, r, txt, text_size = size.tiny,
         text_halign = c == 0 ? text.align_left : text.align_right,
         text_color = head ? color.new(chart.fg_color, 30) : col,
         bgcolor = color.new(chart.bg_color, 5))

if showTable and not cleanMode and barstate.islast and array.size(hDir) > 0
    recCell(0, 0, "#",    chart.fg_color, true)
    recCell(1, 0, "dir",  chart.fg_color, true)
    recCell(2, 0, "got",  chart.fg_color, true)
    recCell(3, 0, "peak", chart.fg_color, true)
    recCell(4, 0, str.tostring(lotSize) + " lot", chart.fg_color, true)
    recCell(5, 0, "exit", chart.fg_color, true)
    n     = array.size(hDir)
    shown = math.min(showHist, math.min(n, 11))
    totR  = 0.0
    totM  = 0.0
    wins  = 0
    nTp   = 0
    nErl  = 0
    nSl   = 0
    totPk = 0.0          // total R that was AVAILABLE at each trade's best
    totLeft = 0.0        // the give-back, in account money
    wentGreen = 0        // trades that were ever in profit at all
    for j = 0 to n - 1
        rr_ = array.get(hR, j)
        wy_ = array.get(hWhy, j)
        pk_ = array.get(hMfe, j)
        totPk += pk_
        totLeft += (pk_ - rr_) * array.get(hRsk, j) * ccyPerPt * (lotSize / 0.01)
        // "hit 1R", not "was ever green": a fill at a bar's OPEN with peak read
        // from that bar's high/low makes peak > 0 on any bar with range, so
        // "% green" would read ~100% regardless of whether the strategy works.
        wentGreen += pk_ >= 1.0 ? 1 : 0
        totR += rr_
        // Split across two statements, not one wrapped line. A wrapped line
        // must NOT be indented by a multiple of 4 - Pine reads that as a new
        // block and raises "end of line without line continuation". At 16
        // spaces this refused to compile, in both files, for five commits.
        mj = (array.get(hExt, j) - array.get(hEnt, j)) * array.get(hDir, j)
        totM += mj * ccyPerPt * (lotSize / 0.01)
        // >= 0 to match the colour the chart already gives a break-even trade
        wins += rr_ >= 0 ? 1 : 0
        nTp  += wy_ == "TP" ? 1 : 0
        nSl  += wy_ == "SL" ? 1 : 0
        nErl += (wy_ == "TRL" or wy_ == "TIME" or wy_ == "FLIP") ? 1 : 0
    for k = 0 to shown - 1
        j  = n - 1 - k
        d_ = array.get(hDir, j)
        r_ = array.get(hR, j)
        m_ = (array.get(hExt, j) - array.get(hEnt, j)) * d_ * ccyPerPt * (lotSize / 0.01)
        col = r_ >= 0 ? accBull : accBear
        pkr = array.get(hMfe, j)
        recCell(0, k + 1, str.tostring(n - k), color.new(chart.fg_color, 45), false)
        recCell(1, k + 1, d_ > 0 ? "L" : "S", col, false)
        recCell(2, k + 1, str.tostring(r_, "#.##"), col, false)
        // amber when the trade gave back most of its best: an execution
        // problem, not a signal problem
        recCell(3, k + 1, str.tostring(pkr, "#.##"),
             pkr > 0 and r_ < pkr * 0.5 ? color.new(#FFB300, 0)
             : color.new(chart.fg_color, 45), false)
        recCell(4, k + 1, (m_ >= 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(m_, "#.##"), col, false)
        recCell(5, k + 1, array.get(hWhy, j), col, false)
    totRow = shown + 1
    // "LAST n", not "TOTAL": the history holds the most recent 30 trades and
    // older ones are shifted out, so every aggregate here is a rolling window.
    recCell(0, totRow, "LAST " + str.tostring(n), chart.fg_color, true)
    recCell(1, totRow, str.tostring(math.round(100.0 * wins / n)) + "%", chart.fg_color, true)
    recCell(2, totRow, str.tostring(totR, "#.#"), totR >= 0 ? accBull : accBear, false)
    recCell(3, totRow, str.tostring(totPk, "#.#"), color.new(chart.fg_color, 45), false)
    recCell(4, totRow, (totM >= 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(totM, "#.##"),
         totM >= 0 ? accBull : accBear, false)
    recCell(5, totRow, "avg " + str.tostring(totR / n, "#.##") + "R",
         chart.fg_color, true)
    // How the trades actually ENDED. If "early" dominates, your target is too
    // far away for this market; if SL dominates, your stop is too close.
    mixRow = totRow + 1
    recCell(0, mixRow, "how they ended", chart.fg_color, true)
    recCell(1, mixRow, "TP " + str.tostring(nTp), accBull, false)
    recCell(2, mixRow, "early " + str.tostring(nErl), color.new(#FFB300, 0), false)
    recCell(3, mixRow, "SL " + str.tostring(nSl), accBear, false)
    recCell(4, mixRow, str.tostring(entryDelay) + "-bar fill", chart.fg_color, true)
    recCell(5, mixRow, "", chart.fg_color, true)

    // ---- THE EXECUTION ROW.
    // "kept" is what was actually banked as a share of what the trades were
    // worth at their best. It separates a signal problem from an execution
    // problem: if most trades go green and "kept" is low, the entries are
    // fine and the exits are throwing the money away.
    exRow = mixRow + 1
    capture = totPk > 0 ? 100.0 * totR / totPk : 0.0
    recCell(0, exRow, "EXECUTION", chart.fg_color, true)
    recCell(1, exRow, str.tostring(math.round(100.0 * wentGreen / n)) + "% hit 1R",
         wentGreen > n / 2 ? accBull : color.new(chart.fg_color, 45), false)
    recCell(2, exRow, "kept " + str.tostring(capture, "#") + "%",
         capture >= 50 ? accBull : capture >= 25 ? color.new(#FFB300, 0) : accBear, false)
    recCell(3, exRow, "left " + str.tostring(totPk - totR, "#.#") + "R",
         color.new(#FFB300, 0), false)
    recCell(4, exRow, "-" + str.tostring(totLeft, "#.##"),
         color.new(#FFB300, 0), false)
    recCell(5, exRow, "left behind", chart.fg_color, true)

// ═══════════════════ IS PRICE REACTING AT A ZONE RIGHT NOW? ═════════════════
// A setup is a reaction that passed every filter. Most reactions never get
// that far, and Veer trades those by eye - so the chart should say when price
// is AT a level and showing a response, even when nothing fires.
atZone      = false
atZonePx    = 0.0
atZoneIsHi  = false
zoneTolNow  = zoneTol * atr
rngNow      = math.max(high - low, syminfo.mintick)
upWickNow   = high - math.max(open, close)
dnWickNow   = math.min(open, close) - low

scanAt(array<float> px, array<bool> dead, bool isHigh) =>
    float found = na
    if array.size(px) > 0
        for i = 0 to array.size(px) - 1
            if not array.get(dead, i) and na(found)
                p = array.get(px, i)
                if low <= p + zoneTolNow and high >= p - zoneTolNow
                    found := p
    found

atHi = scanAt(hiPx, hiDead, true)
atLo = scanAt(loPx, loDead, false)
if not na(atHi)
    atZone := true
    atZonePx := atHi
    atZoneIsHi := true
else if not na(atLo)
    atZone := true
    atZonePx := atLo
    atZoneIsHi := false

// a rejection is a wick that goes into the zone and a close that comes back out
rejecting = atZone and (atZoneIsHi ? (upWickNow / rngNow) >= 0.30 and close < atZonePx
                                   : (dnWickNow / rngNow) >= 0.30 and close > atZonePx)

// Every rejection at a level, whether or not it survives the filters. Veer
// trades these by eye and the chart should not stay silent about them just
// because the automated setup did not qualify. Declared here, after the
// detector, because Pine is single-pass and a plot cannot read a value that is
// defined further down the file.
plotshape(rejecting and not atZoneIsHi, "reaction at support", shape.circle,
     location.belowbar, color.new(accBull, 30), size = size.tiny)
plotshape(rejecting and atZoneIsHi, "reaction at resistance", shape.circle,
     location.abovebar, color.new(accBear, 30), size = size.tiny)

// ═══════════════════ THE ORDER BOOK — where to actually place ═══════════════
// "sell limits buy limits stops if it can produce that".
//
// Four orders, derived from the nearest LIVE level on each side of price:
//   BUY LIMIT   at support        - buy the level, betting it holds
//   SELL LIMIT  at resistance     - sell the level, betting it holds
//   BUY STOP    above resistance  - buy the break, betting it does not
//   SELL STOP   below support     - sell the break
//
// The limits and the stops are opposite bets on the same two prices, which is
// the point: whichever way the level resolves, the order for it is already
// worked out. Each row carries its stop, its target, the R:R that implies, and
// what the zone did the last times price was there - so a row with a bad R:R
// or a zone that has broken three times can be skipped on sight.
showOrders = input.bool(true, "Show the order book (limits and stops)", group = gD)
orderBuf   = input.float(0.15, "   place stops this far beyond the level (× ATR)",
     minval = 0.02, step = 0.01, group = gD,
     tooltip = "A buy stop sitting exactly on the level gets filled by the wick that tests it. This pushes it far enough beyond that only a real break triggers it.")

var table obook = table.new(position.top_left, 6, 5, frame_width = 1,
     frame_color = color.new(color.gray, 60), border_width = 1,
     border_color = color.new(color.gray, 85))

oCell(int c, int r, string txt, color col, bool head) =>
    table.cell(obook, c, r, txt, text_size = size.tiny,
         text_halign = c == 0 ? text.align_left : text.align_right,
         text_color = head ? color.new(chart.fg_color, 30) : col,
         bgcolor = color.new(chart.bg_color, 5))

// Both the lines AND the labels are tracked. barstate.islast re-runs on every
// tick of the forming bar, so anything drawn here without being deleted first
// accumulates once per tick until the drawing cap evicts something else.
var array<line>  oLines = array.new_line()
var array<label> oLabs  = array.new_label()

clearOrders() =>
    while array.size(oLines) > 0
        l9 = array.shift(oLines)
        if not na(l9)
            line.delete(l9)
    while array.size(oLabs) > 0
        b9 = array.shift(oLabs)
        if not na(b9)
            label.delete(b9)
    0

drawOrder(float px, string txt, color col) =>
    array.push(oLines, line.new(bar_index, px, bar_index + 12, px,
         color = col, style = line.style_dotted, width = 1))
    array.push(oLabs, label.new(bar_index + 12, px, txt,
         style = label.style_label_left, color = color.new(color.white, 100),
         textcolor = col, size = size.tiny))
    0

// A Pine function CANNOT be declared inside an if block - it must live at
// global scope. Defining this one inside the `if showOrders` body would not
// have compiled.
oRow(int r, string nm, float en, float sl2, float tp2, color col,
     float zp, bool zhi) =>
    if na(en) or na(sl2) or na(tp2)
        oCell(0, r, nm, color.new(chart.fg_color, 65), false)
        for cc = 1 to 5
            oCell(cc, r, "-", color.new(chart.fg_color, 65), false)
    else
        rk2 = math.abs(en - sl2)
        rr2 = rk2 > 0 ? math.abs(tp2 - en) / rk2 : 0.0
        zt2 = zoneText(zp, zhi)
        oCell(0, r, nm, col, false)
        oCell(1, r, str.tostring(en, format.mintick), col, false)
        oCell(2, r, str.tostring(sl2, format.mintick), color.new(chart.fg_color, 40), false)
        oCell(3, r, str.tostring(tp2, format.mintick), color.new(chart.fg_color, 40), false)
        oCell(4, r, str.tostring(rr2, "#.#"),
             rr2 >= 2 ? accBull : rr2 >= 1 ? color.new(#FFB300, 0) : accBear, false)
        oCell(5, r, zt2 == "" ? "no record" : zt2, color.new(chart.fg_color, 40), false)
        drawOrder(en, nm + "  " + str.tostring(en, format.mintick), col)
    0

if showOrders and barstate.islast
    clearOrders()
    res = nextLiq(true)
    sup = nextLiq(false)
    buf = orderBuf * atr

    oCell(0, 0, "order",  chart.fg_color, true)
    oCell(1, 0, "entry",  chart.fg_color, true)
    oCell(2, 0, "stop",   chart.fg_color, true)
    oCell(3, 0, "target", chart.fg_color, true)
    oCell(4, 0, "R:R",    chart.fg_color, true)
    oCell(5, 0, "this zone before", chart.fg_color, true)

    oRow(1, "BUY LIMIT",  sup, na(sup) ? na : sup - slBuf * atr, res, accBull, sup, false)
    oRow(2, "SELL LIMIT", res, na(res) ? na : res + slBuf * atr, sup, accBear, res, true)
    oRow(3, "BUY STOP",   na(res) ? na : res + buf,
         na(res) ? na : res - slBuf * atr,
         na(res) or na(sup) ? na : res + buf + (res - sup), accBull, res, true)
    oRow(4, "SELL STOP",  na(sup) ? na : sup - buf,
         na(sup) ? na : sup + slBuf * atr,
         na(res) or na(sup) ? na : sup - buf - (res - sup), accBear, sup, false)

// ══════════════════════════ WHAT TO DO RIGHT NOW ════════════════════════════
// One line, top centre, in words. Everything else on this chart is detail you
// can ignore; this is the answer. It reads, in priority order:
//   a signal firing now  ->  the trade, with entry, stop, target and size
//   price at a zone      ->  which zone, its record, and what to watch for
//   otherwise            ->  the nearest order and how far away it is
showNow = input.bool(true, "Show the WHAT TO DO NOW line", group = gD)

var table nowbox = table.new(position.top_center, 1, 2, frame_width = 2,
     frame_color = color.new(color.gray, 45), border_width = 1,
     border_color = color.new(color.gray, 80))

if showNow and barstate.islast
    string headline = ""
    string detail   = ""
    color  tone     = color.new(chart.fg_color, 25)

    nPend2 = array.size(pdDir)
    nOpen2 = array.size(trDir)

    if nOpen2 > 0
        d0 = array.get(trDir, 0)
        e0 = array.get(trEnt, 0)
        s0 = array.get(trSl, 0)
        t0 = array.get(trTp, 0)
        m0 = (close - e0) * d0 * ccyPerPt * (lotSize / 0.01)
        headline := (d0 > 0 ? "IN A BUY" : "IN A SELL") + "   "
             + (m0 >= 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(m0, "#.##")
        detail := "from " + str.tostring(e0, format.mintick)
             + "   stop " + str.tostring(s0, format.mintick)
             + "   target " + str.tostring(t0, format.mintick)
        tone := m0 >= 0 ? accBull : accBear
    else if nPend2 > 0
        d1 = array.get(pdDir, 0)
        r1 = array.get(pdRisk, 0)
        g1 = array.get(pdSig, 0)
        money1 = r1 * ccyPerPt * (lotSize / 0.01)
        headline := d1 > 0 ? "BUY NOW" : "SELL NOW"
        detail := array.get(pdSet, 0) + "   signal " + str.tostring(g1, format.mintick)
             + "   stop " + str.tostring(d1 > 0 ? g1 - r1 : g1 + r1, format.mintick)
             + "   risking " + str.tostring(money1, "#.##") + " at " + str.tostring(lotSize)
        tone := d1 > 0 ? accBull : accBear
    else if rejecting
        headline := atZoneIsHi ? "REJECTING RESISTANCE" : "REJECTING SUPPORT"
        zt3 = zoneText(atZonePx, atZoneIsHi)
        detail := (atZoneIsHi ? "SSL " : "BSL ") + str.tostring(atZonePx, format.mintick)
             + "   " + (zt3 == "" ? "no record here" : zt3)
             + "   " + (atZoneIsHi ? "watch for a sell" : "watch for a buy")
        tone := atZoneIsHi ? accBear : accBull
    else if atZone
        headline := "AT A ZONE - WAIT FOR THE REACTION"
        zt4 = zoneText(atZonePx, atZoneIsHi)
        detail := (atZoneIsHi ? "SSL " : "BSL ") + str.tostring(atZonePx, format.mintick)
             + "   " + (zt4 == "" ? "no record here" : zt4)
        tone := color.new(#FFB300, 0)
    else
        res2 = nextLiq(true)
        sup2 = nextLiq(false)
        dUp = na(res2) ? na : res2 - close
        dDn = na(sup2) ? na : close - sup2
        headline := "NOTHING YET - PRICE IS BETWEEN LEVELS"
        detail := (na(res2) ? "no level above" :
             "sell zone " + str.tostring(res2, format.mintick)
             + " (" + str.tostring(dUp, "#.#") + " away)")
             + "     " +
             (na(sup2) ? "no level below" :
             "buy zone " + str.tostring(sup2, format.mintick)
             + " (" + str.tostring(dDn, "#.#") + " away)")
        tone := color.new(chart.fg_color, 30)

    table.cell(nowbox, 0, 0, headline, text_size = size.large,
         text_color = tone, bgcolor = color.new(chart.bg_color, 5))
    table.cell(nowbox, 0, 1, detail, text_size = size.small,
         text_color = color.new(chart.fg_color, 25),
         bgcolor = color.new(chart.bg_color, 5))

// ══════════════════ WHICH SETUP IS ACTUALLY PAYING YOU ══════════════════════
// The question this answers is "I do not know which setups to take".
//
// It cannot be answered from history. A confluence score built from every
// factor already measured in this repo separated winners from losers in only
// 5 of 8 markets and ran BACKWARDS on gold 15m. The one factor with a real
// edge, ADX < 35, is already on by default. There is no hidden ranking waiting
// in the past to be found.
//
// So the ranking comes from YOUR results. Every completed trade is filed under
// the setup that produced it, and this is the scoreboard. After a week of live
// trading it says, in your own money, which types to keep taking and which to
// switch off - the only version of the question that can actually be answered.
showBoard = input.bool(true, "Show the setup scoreboard", group = gD)

var table board = table.new(position.bottom_left, 5, 10, frame_width = 1,
     frame_color = color.new(color.gray, 60), border_width = 1,
     border_color = color.new(color.gray, 85))

bCell(int c, int r, string txt, color col, bool head) =>
    table.cell(board, c, r, txt, text_size = size.tiny,
         text_halign = c == 0 ? text.align_left : text.align_right,
         text_color = head ? color.new(chart.fg_color, 30) : col,
         bgcolor = color.new(chart.bg_color, 5))

kinds = array.from("BREAK", "BOUNCE", "RETEST", "PULLBACK", "BREAKOUT",
     "GAP FILL", "INVERSE GAP", "ORDER BLOCK")

if showBoard and not cleanMode and barstate.islast and array.size(hSet) > 0
    bCell(0, 0, "setup",  chart.fg_color, true)
    bCell(1, 0, "taken",  chart.fg_color, true)
    bCell(2, 0, "win",    chart.fg_color, true)
    bCell(3, 0, "R",      chart.fg_color, true)
    bCell(4, 0, str.tostring(lotSize) + " lot", chart.fg_color, true)
    for ki = 0 to array.size(kinds) - 1
        kd = array.get(kinds, ki)
        int   cnt = 0
        int   wn  = 0
        float sr  = 0.0
        float sm  = 0.0
        for j = 0 to array.size(hSet) - 1
            if array.get(hSet, j) == kd
                cnt += 1
                rj = array.get(hR, j)
                sr += rj
                wn += rj >= 0 ? 1 : 0
                mj2 = (array.get(hExt, j) - array.get(hEnt, j)) * array.get(hDir, j)
                sm += mj2 * ccyPerPt * (lotSize / 0.01)
        dimc = color.new(chart.fg_color, 60)
        col  = cnt == 0 ? dimc
             : sm > 0 ? accBull : sm < 0 ? accBear : color.new(chart.fg_color, 45)
        bCell(0, ki + 1, kd, cnt == 0 ? dimc : chart.fg_color, false)
        // amber until the count can support a conclusion
        bCell(1, ki + 1, cnt == 0 ? "-" : str.tostring(cnt),
             cnt == 0 ? dimc : cnt < 53 ? color.new(#FFB300, 0) : col, false)
        bCell(2, ki + 1, cnt == 0 ? "-"
             : str.tostring(math.round(100.0 * wn / cnt)) + "%", col, false)
        bCell(3, ki + 1, cnt == 0 ? "-" : str.tostring(sr, "#.#"), col, false)
        bCell(4, ki + 1, cnt == 0 ? "-"
             : (sm >= 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(sm, "#.##"), col, false)
    // E-046: 53 trades per type before "this one is catastrophic" is
    // distinguishable from noise, and 371 before "this one makes +0.25R" is.
    // Showing the count without that context invites reading four trades as a
    // verdict, which is how a scoreboard becomes worse than no scoreboard.
    bCell(0, 9, "need 53+ each to judge", color.new(chart.fg_color, 30), true)
    bCell(1, 9, "", chart.fg_color, true)
    bCell(2, 9, "", chart.fg_color, true)
    bCell(3, 9, "", chart.fg_color, true)
    bCell(4, 9, "switch off red", color.new(chart.fg_color, 30), true)

// ──────────────────────────────────── ALERTS ────────────────────────────────
// Alerts fire on firedLong/firedShort - the direction actually TAKEN - not on
// canFire and goLong. With "wait for a retest" on, the trade direction comes
// from the armed setup, which can differ from goLong on the retest bar: the
// old condition could alert BUY on a bar the indicator went short.
alertcondition(firedLong,  "Liquidity Sniper BUY",
     "LiqSniper BUY {{ticker}} {{interval}} at {{close}}")
alertcondition(firedShort, "Liquidity Sniper SELL",
     "LiqSniper SELL {{ticker}} {{interval}} at {{close}}")
alertcondition(firedLong or firedShort, "Liquidity Sniper ANY",
     "LiqSniper signal {{ticker}} {{interval}} at {{close}}")
